*Gift cards** are delivered by email and cannot be cancelled after they are sent. See the Gift Card Terms.\n\n## After delivery: tell us what went wrong\n\nWhen an order has been delivered, there is no self-service return or refund. Instead:\n\n1. Go to **Your account > Get help with an order** (or open the order and choose **Get help with this order**).\n2. Choose the order, and the package if there is more than one.\n3. Choose what went wrong: *I didn''t receive it*, *It arrived damaged*, *I got the wrong item*, *It''s not as described*, or *Something else*.\n4. Describe what you expected and what you got, in your own words.\n5. Add photos. JPEG, PNG, WebP and HEIC photos are accepted. Photos of the item, the packaging, any label and anything that shows the problem help us decide faster. For items that did not arrive, a photo of the delivery location or any delivery notice helps.\n6. Send the request. You get a reference number, and we reply on your help requests page and by your notification channels.\n\nPlease report a problem as soon as you see it. For perishable food, report it on the day it arrives, and keep the item and packaging until we reply, unless it is unsafe to keep. [[LEGAL: Q13 consider and confirm any time limit to report a problem; the product requirement does not set one and an unlimited window is a business risk]]\n\nOur team reviews your description, your photos, the delivery record (including the delivery code and proof of delivery), the seller''s information and the order history. We may ask you for more detail or more photos. We may speak to the seller or the carrier. We aim to reply within [[LEGAL: Q32 reply target in business days, to be set by Support operations]]. Request statuses are Received, In review, Resolved, Closed or Withdrawn, and you can withdraw a request at any time.\n\n**What we can decide.** Depending on the problem, our team may refund all or part of the order, replace the item, send a missing item, give you store credit, or decide that no refund is due. We will tell you our decision and the reason. You do not have to send items back unless we ask; if we do ask, we will pay for the return shipping and tell you how. Where an item is perishable, unsafe or not worth the cost of collecting, we may let you keep or dispose of it.\n\n**What we need from you.** The facts must be true. We may refuse requests that are false, that come from a pattern of abuse, or for problems that are not covered, for example a change of mind, a wrong size you ordered, damage after delivery, or an item you used. We may take account of how often an account asks for help. Repeated false claims can lead to a limit or closure of the account under the Conditions of Use.\n\n## How refunds are paid\n\nA refund goes back to each person or method that paid, in the same way they paid.\n\n- **The card (or other cash payment method) part** of the order goes back to that card or method. We cannot refund a card payment to a different card, to cash or to a person. Bank and card processing times apply: card refunds usually show up on your statement 5 to 10 business days after we issue them; bank (ACH) refunds can take up to 7 business days. [[LEGAL: Q13 confirm the refund timing statements]]\n- **The store credit part** of the order goes back as store credit, with the same value, to the account that used it. See the Store Credit Terms.\n- **A gift card used to pay** was turned into store credit when it was redeemed, so that part is returned as store credit.\n- **If more than one person paid,** each payer gets back what they paid.\n- **Delivery fees and tax** are included when the whole order is refunded. For a partial refund we say what is covered.\n- **Membership fees** have their own refund rule in the Plus Membership Terms.\n\nYou cannot choose to have the card part refunded as credit, and the product has no such option. Only our staff, inside a help decision and with a recorded reason, may send the card part to your store credit instead of the card. They might do so, for example, if the card is closed and the refund would be returned. This is one way: credit is never turned back into a card refund. If a card refund fails, we will contact you to agree what to do.\n\n## Credit as a goodwill gesture\n\nStaff may add store credit to your account as a goodwill gesture or to settle a request, with a reason that is recorded. That credit follows the Store Credit Terms: it can only buy products and services and can never be turned into cash.\n\n## Items you should not use or open\n\nPerishable food, opened beauty and personal-care items, custom-made clothing and gift cards are not good candidates for a replacement, because they cannot safely be resold. We will still look at a request if one of these arrives damaged, defective, unsafe, not as described, or does not arrive.\n\n## Recalls and safety\n\nIf an item you bought is recalled or found unsafe, we will tell you what to do. You will be refunded for recalled items as required by the recall or by the law.\n\n## Your legal rights\n\nNothing on this page limits any right you have under federal or state law that cannot be waived, including rights about goods that are defective, not as described, not delivered, or delivered late, and any right to a refund or replacement under a statute, a card-network rule or a manufacturer''s warranty. You may also ask your card issuer or bank to look at a charge, as the issuer''s rules allow, and you can contact your state attorney general or consumer protection office. We ask you to contact us first, because we can usually fix a problem quickly. [[LEGAL: Q13 and Q23 verify this policy and the required disclosures against the refund-policy and consumer-protection laws of each launch state, FTC Mail, Internet and Telephone Order Merchandise Rule requirements, and the sale of perishables]]\n\nSome goods come with a manufacturer''s or seller''s warranty. If a seller offers its own return or warranty terms on a listing, those are extra rights; they do not reduce your rights here.\n\n## Services\n\nRefunds for services follow the provider''s cancellation policy shown before booking. If a provider cancels or does not show up, you get a full refund, including any deposit. If a service was not performed as promised, use Your account > Get help with an order in the same way as for a product.\n\n## Contact\n\nHelp: Your account > Get help with an order. Phone: {phone}. Email: {legal}. Accessibility help: {accessibility}. Address: {address}."}'::jsonb, change_summary = 'Replaces the earlier 30-day self-service return policy. Delivered orders now have no self-service return or refund; customers report a problem with photos and our team decides.', created_at = now(), updated_at = now()
    where doc_type = 'returns' and (major, minor, patch) = (1, 0, 0) and status = 'CURRENT'
      and created_by in ('seed', 'legal-loader') and updated_at = created_at;

-- seller-terms 1.0 (effective 2026-12-01; locales: en; draft banner on)
delete from legal_version where doc_type = 'seller-terms' and created_by = 'seed'
    and exists (select 1 from legal_version c where c.doc_type = 'seller-terms' and c.status = 'CURRENT'
                and c.created_by = 'seed' and c.updated_at = c.created_at and (c.major, c.minor, c.patch) > (1, 0, 0));
update legal_version set status = 'ARCHIVED', archived_at = now(), updated_at = now()
    where doc_type = 'seller-terms' and status = 'CURRENT' and (major, minor, patch) < (1, 0, 0);
insert into legal_version (id, doc_type, version, major, minor, patch, effective_date, status, draft_banner, bodies,
                           change_summary, created_at, created_by, updated_at, archived_at)
select 'ecd30d35-c0c0-59fe-8232-d15b91e9b67e'::uuid, 'seller-terms', '1.0', 1, 0, 0, '2026-12-01'::date, 'CURRENT', true,
       '{"en": "# Seller Terms\n\n> DRAFT. This document was prepared with AI assistance and has not been reviewed by qualified counsel. It must be reviewed and approved by counsel before it is published or relied on. Text in double square brackets marked LEGAL is an open decision.\n\n## 1. Agreement and definitions\n\n1.1 These Seller Terms (the \"Terms\") are a contract between you (the business or individual that applies for or holds a seller account, \"Seller\", \"you\") and [[LEGAL: contracting entity name, form, state of organisation and registered address for AfroTransact]] (\"AfroTransact\", \"we\", \"us\").\n\n1.2 The following documents form part of the agreement and are incorporated by reference. If they conflict, the order of priority is: the Payment Terms (for anything about money movement, Stripe, payouts, holds and negative balances); the Data Processing Terms (for personal data); these Terms; the Seller Fees Summary; then any policy page we link to.\n- Payment Terms for Sellers and Providers (\"Payment Terms\")\n- Data Processing Terms (\"Data Processing Terms\")\n- Seller Fees Summary and the current fee schedule shown in Seller Center\n- Sub-processors list\n- Prohibited & Restricted Items policy, Intellectual Property policy and Community Guidelines\n- Returns & Refunds policy (as it applies to customers) and the Privacy Notice\n\n1.3 Capitalised terms: \"Marketplace\" means AfroTransact''s websites, apps and APIs. \"Seller Center\" is your back office. \"Order\" is a customer purchase of products. \"Shipment\" is the part of an Order fulfilled from one location by one fulfilment method. \"Delivered\" means the time a Shipment is recorded as handed over under section 9. \"Hold Window\" means the settlement hold in section 14. \"Customer\" is a shopper. \"AfroTransact Retail\" is AfroTransact''s own first-party retail business, which sells on the Marketplace as a seller of record. \"Staff\" means authorised AfroTransact personnel.\n\n1.4 If you offer services or bookings, the Service Provider Terms also apply to that part of your activity. If you do both, both documents apply and the Service Provider Terms control for services.\n\n1.5 By submitting an application, accepting these Terms in Seller Center or listing a product, you agree to them. If you accept for a business, you confirm that you are authorised to bind it.\n\n## 2. Eligibility and onboarding review\n\n2.1 To sell you must: be at least 18 and have legal capacity; be a legal entity or an individual (sole proprietor) able to enter binding contracts; operate in a market that is live on the Marketplace (we will tell you which); hold every licence, permit, registration and insurance required for what you sell (for example food permits, cosmetic labelling compliance and any state or local licences); and not be subject to sanctions or located in or ordinarily resident in an embargoed jurisdiction.\n\n2.2 Application. You complete a resumable application covering business details, owners and persons with significant control, tax information, payout and identity verification, plan choice, and catalogue compliance information (such as permits for food or perishables). All information must be accurate, complete and kept up to date. We may ask for documents and for more information at any time.\n\n2.3 Two-step review. Every application is reviewed in two separate steps by two different members of Staff: a first review of documents and checks, and a final approval by a second person. Either step may approve, request more information, flag the application, send it back or reject it. We record the reason for each decision. We are not required to approve any application and may decline for any lawful reason, including risk, prohibited categories, sanctions or screening results, duplicate accounts, reputation or incomplete information.\n\n2.4 Identity and business verification is performed by Stripe, our payment processor, as described in the Payment Terms. You must pass Stripe''s verification and keep your connected account in good standing. If Stripe restricts your account, we may hide your listings and pause payouts under section 21.\n\n2.5 Approval is for the offering we approve (for example products, services or both) and the categories we approve. You may not sell in a restricted category without our specific approval.\n\n2.6 Ongoing eligibility. You must tell us promptly of any change to ownership, control, legal name, address, licences, permits, insurance, tax status or anything else that could affect these answers. Expired or revoked licences and permits may result in listings being hidden.\n\n## 3. Accounts, roles and security\n\n3.1 Your account has an owner and may have members with roles such as admin, catalogue, finance, fulfilment, support and staff. The owner is responsible for who is invited, for the permissions they hold, and for removing people who leave.\n\n3.2 You are responsible for everything done through your account and by your members, agents and integrations. Keep credentials secret, use the multi-factor options we offer and tell us immediately of any suspected compromise.\n\n3.3 One person may hold customer, seller and provider roles on one sign-in. Your seller activity is separate from your activity as a shopper, and you may not use a seller account to buy your own products, to inflate sales, reviews or ratings, or to circumvent a restriction.\n\n3.4 Only owners and finance members may change payout or plan settings and open Stripe account management. You must keep at least one active owner at all times.\n\n## 4. Listings and product rules\n\n4.1 You must only list products you are lawfully entitled to sell and have in stock or can fulfil by the stated date. A listing must be accurate, complete and not misleading. It must include the true condition, quantity, ingredients and allergens where relevant, country of origin where required, size or weight, and any warnings.\n\n4.2 Food, supplements, cosmetics and other regulated goods must comply with all laws that apply to the sale and shipment of that product, including labelling, registration, permits, shelf life, cold-chain and import rules. Perishables must be shipped or delivered in a way that keeps them safe. You are responsible for recalls and must tell us immediately if a product you sold is recalled or unsafe.\n\n4.3 Listing content you or a tool prepare (including text generated with our AI listing tools) remains your responsibility. You must review it before publishing.\n\n4.4 Listings are moderated. We may review, hold, edit the presentation of, hide or remove a listing at any time, including automatically, for policy, safety, legal, quality or risk reasons. We may place listings in a moderation queue before they go live.\n\n4.5 Product identity. We match your listing to the catalogue where possible. We may merge or de-duplicate listings, assign identifiers, and apply category and tax classification. You must answer tax-related listing questions accurately.\n\n4.6 You may not: list a prohibited item; list the same product in a misleading way to avoid detection; use another seller''s content or images without permission; use competitor names or counterfeit marks; solicit customers to buy or pay outside the Marketplace; ask Customers for off-platform contact or payment; or collect Customer information for any purpose other than fulfilling the Order and your legal obligations.\n\n4.7 Inventory. If you lose inventory or cannot fulfil, you must update or withdraw the listing promptly. Repeated stock-outs after the order is placed are counted as cancellations under section 10.\n\n## 5. Pricing\n\n5.1 You set your prices in US dollars unless we enable other currencies. Prices must be the total price of the product before taxes and any delivery fee that we show separately. Customer-facing prices must be honest and must not be inflated on the Marketplace to cover fees in a way our policies prohibit.\n\n5.2 We may display discounts, coupons, credits, membership benefits, or promotions funded by us or by you. Promotions you fund reduce your earnings only as you agreed when you created the promotion. Promotions we fund are not deducted from your earnings unless we told you so in the promotion terms.\n\n5.3 We calculate and show shipping, delivery fees and taxes at checkout according to rules that we configure (see section 8 and section 16). You may not add hidden surcharges.\n\n5.4 A price error is your responsibility. We may cancel or correct an Order where the displayed price was clearly wrong, subject to our policies and applicable law.\n\n## 6. Fees, commission and plan fees\n\n6.1 Types of fees. You pay AfroTransact (a) a plan fee for your chosen plan, if any; (b) a commission, as a percentage of each product sale; (c) for services only, a connection fee (see the Service Provider Terms); (d) fees for optional or required services such as fulfilment by AfroTransact, shipping labels bought through us, handling, and delivery fees where the delivery fee rule assigns them to the seller; and (e) the Stripe processing and payout fees that Stripe charges, which are borne as described in the Payment Terms.\n\n6.2 Schedules are configured, not fixed in these Terms. The rates and amounts are set in fee schedules and plan catalogues that AfroTransact Staff configure. They may depend on your plan, the product category, rates we negotiate with you in writing, and the delivery method. The Seller Fees Summary explains how this works. The rate that applies to a sale is recorded when the order is placed and does not change for that order if we later change the schedule.\n\n6.3 Where to see them. The fee schedule that applies to you, your plan price and any negotiated rate and its end date are shown in Seller Center. A statement shows commission and fees per order line.\n\n6.4 Changes. We may change fees, plan prices and schedules by giving at least thirty (30) days'' notice [[LEGAL: confirm minimum notice period for fee changes and whether shorter notice is acceptable for changes that benefit sellers or are required by law or by Stripe]]. A change takes effect for new orders after the notice period. Existing plan subscribers stay on their plan version until their next renewal unless they agree to migrate sooner. If you do not agree to a change you may stop selling and close your account before it takes effect.\n\n6.5 Plan trials, free periods and rewards. We may offer trials, free months, credits, rewards, or charge conditions (for example, a plan that is free until certain sales or listing thresholds are reached). These are subject to their stated conditions, are decided by the rules stated when offered, are given once per seller unless stated otherwise, and may be withdrawn for abuse or misrepresentation.\n\n6.6 AfroTransact Retail does not pay commission or plan fees to AfroTransact. This does not affect your fees.\n\n6.7 Refunds and fee treatment. On a refund, the commission is returned to you in the proportion the schedule provides for. Delivery fees, label costs, processing costs and other fees may be kept or returned depending on who was at fault, as described in the Seller Fees Summary.\n\n## 7. Billing waterfall for plan fees\n\n7.1 Your plan fee is billed through our billing provider on the cycle you choose (monthly, quarterly or annual, as offered).\n\n7.2 Order of settlement. When an invoice for a plan fee falls due, it is satisfied in this order:\n1. first, by any billing credit, reward, coupon, trial or free-period balance on your account;\n2. second, if you have opted in to \"pay your plan from your sales\" and the feature is switched on for your account, from your pending earnings that are eligible (delivered, undisputed and not yet transferred); and\n3. third, by charging your card or bank account on file for any remaining balance.\n\n7.3 If you do not opt in, steps 1 and 3 apply. You may change your choice in Seller Center; a change applies from the next invoice, not to an invoice already created. We may switch the earnings option off at any time, in which case invoices are charged to your card.\n\n7.4 Earnings withheld for a plan fee are not held for any other reason. If the invoice is voided, credited or refunded, the withheld amount is released and paid to you with a later transfer.\n\n7.5 If a charge fails we may retry, suspend plan benefits or features, downgrade you to a plan without those features, or suspend your account after notice as stated in section 21.\n\n7.6 Invoices and receipts are available in Seller Center.\n\n## 8. Orders and fulfilment obligations\n\n8.1 When a Customer places an Order that includes your products, a contract of sale is formed between you and the Customer (or, for AfroTransact Retail, between AfroTransact Retail and the Customer). You are the seller of record. AfroTransact is not a party to your sale contract except where it sells as AfroTransact Retail. We provide the Marketplace and payment and related services.\n\n8.2 You must accept, pack and hand over each Order by the ship-by or ready-by time shown in Seller Center. You must pack items to prevent damage, keep perishables safe, and include the correct goods in the correct quantity.\n\n8.3 Fulfilment methods. Depending on your account and the listing, an Order may be fulfilled by:\n- the seller''s own shipping through a carrier you choose or through carrier labels bought via the Marketplace;\n- carrier shipping using labels bought through AfroTransact and charged to you at the rate shown, including any handling charge;\n- fulfilment by AfroTransact (stock you have sent to AfroTransact), under the applicable inbound and fulfilment terms and fees;\n- a delivery run, where AfroTransact collects from you or delivers from its own inventory for a configured place and date, with fees as configured;\n- pickup by the Customer from your location or a pickup point; or\n- delivery by you or your own courier.\n\n8.4 Delivery runs. For runs, AfroTransact or its drivers may collect your goods at a time we tell you. A run may be cancelled or extended by Staff, for example if its minimum order count is not met. When a run is cancelled, all Orders on it are cancelled and refunded in full; we will tell you. You are not owed lost sales for a cancelled run [[LEGAL: confirm that seller compensation is excluded]]. Delivery fees on runs are configured by Staff and shown at checkout; the fee rule applied is recorded on the Order.\n\n8.5 Performance. We measure late shipment rate, seller cancellation rate, order defect rate and response times over a rolling period against targets we publish. Below-target accounts may be limited (for example in search or buy-box placement), suspended or terminated under section 21.\n\n8.6 Customer communications. Use Marketplace messaging for Order communications. You must respond to support and claim requests within the time shown. You may not market to Customers or direct them off-platform.\n\n8.7 Packaging and documents. Do not include advertising or off-platform solicitations. Use the packing slip we provide where required.\n\n8.8 You are responsible for loss and damage until Delivery except where the Marketplace expressly provides otherwise for AfroTransact-handled shipments.\n\n## 9. Shipping, delivery proof and codes\n\n9.1 Tracking. Carrier shipments must be shipped with valid tracking, and tracking must show delivery to the address on the Order. Mark shipments shipped only when handed to the carrier.\n\n9.2 Delivery is recorded when: the carrier reports delivery; an AfroTransact driver completes delivery with proof (a photo and handover method, and for hand-to-customer and perishable deliveries a code or signature as required); the Customer collects a pickup order and the Customer''s four-digit handover code is entered or checked; or Staff confirm delivery with a recorded reason.\n\n9.3 Handover codes. Each pickup order and each hand-to-customer or perishable driver delivery has a code the Customer sees on their order. For pickup and seller-delivered orders you must ask for the code and must not hand over without it unless Staff instruct otherwise. You must never ask Customers to share the code before handover, send it to others or write it on a package.\n\n9.4 Proof. Keep evidence of dispatch and delivery (receipts, tracking, photos) for the retention period in section 26. Where AfroTransact collects proof, it is shown to you, the Customer and Staff on the order.\n\n9.5 Seller delivery. If you deliver yourself or through your own courier you are responsible for the courier, for safe handling and for obtaining proof as the Marketplace requires. You warrant that you are authorised and insured to do so.\n\n9.6 Delivery estimates and customers'' preferences (such as safe place) must be followed where reasonable. Safe-place delivery is at the risk stated in the order.\n\n9.7 Risk of loss for carrier and seller deliveries passes to the Customer on Delivery. If a shipment is not delivered, is damaged or is wrong, the dispute process in section 11 and the claims process in section 12 apply.\n\n## 10. Cancellations\n\n10.1 Customer cancellations. Customers may cancel before packing and within the cancellation window shown at checkout. You must not pack or ship an Order that has been cancelled. An authorisation not yet captured is released; a captured payment is refunded.\n\n10.2 Seller cancellations. You may cancel only for a legitimate reason (for example stock error or a safety issue) and must do so as soon as you know. Cancellations by you or by the system because you did not ship count against your performance and may be subject to fees, including recovery of costs we incurred such as a wasted trip or label.\n\n10.3 Staff cancellations. Staff may cancel any Order for fraud, risk, legal or operational reasons.\n\n10.4 Cancellation after pickup. If an Order is cancelled after a delivery trip or label cost is incurred, the cost may be allocated to the party at fault, as set out in the Seller Fees Summary.\n\n## 11. No self-service returns for delivered orders; support and evidence disputes\n\n11.1 AfroTransact does not offer Customers a self-service return flow for Delivered Orders. A Customer who has a problem with a Delivered Order (for example not received, damaged, wrong item, not as described, or quality problem) opens a help request with a description and photo or other evidence. Our Staff decide the outcome.\n\n11.2 Seller role. We may notify you and ask you to respond with your account and evidence within the response window shown on the request. If you do not respond in time, Staff may decide without your response.\n\n11.3 Staff decide. Staff may approve a full or partial refund, a replacement, a credit, no remedy, or another outcome, based on the evidence, the listing, delivery proof, the policies and applicable law. The decision and reasons are recorded and shown to the parties. Staff may ask for more evidence and may escalate. Decisions are made at Staff discretion acting reasonably and are final within the Marketplace, subject to the appeal in 11.7.\n\n11.4 Seller liability. If the outcome is a refund and the problem is attributable to you (for example wrong, damaged, missing or not-as-described goods, late or non-delivery, or breach of these Terms), the refund is charged to you and recovered under section 15. Where the problem is attributable to a carrier or to AfroTransact, we may bear it. A refund may be returned to the Customer to the original payment method or, where Staff decide inside a support resolution, as AfroTransact credit; the way a refund is returned to the Customer does not change what you are charged, unless we tell you otherwise.\n\n11.5 Returned goods. Staff may instruct that goods be returned to you, held, or disposed of (for example perishables). We are not required to require a return before refunding. You must not demand that Customers return goods outside the Marketplace.\n\n11.6 Evidence. You must provide true and complete evidence. Fabricated or misleading evidence is grounds for termination and recovery of losses.\n\n11.7 Appeal. You may ask for a review of a decision through support within the period stated in the decision, giving new evidence. A second Staff member will review. [[LEGAL: confirm whether the appeal process should be offered at all and its time limit]]\n\n11.8 Nothing in this section limits rights a Customer has under consumer law.\n\n## 12. Claims and the A-to-z style guarantee\n\n12.1 AfroTransact provides a Customer guarantee for Orders placed on the Marketplace when something goes wrong (for example a product is not received, is damaged, or is not what was described). Claims are opened by Customers with evidence and decided by Staff.\n\n12.2 Process. A claim moves through: awaiting your response; under review; and then resolved with a refund or denial, or escalated. A claim has response and decision targets. If you do not respond in time, the claim proceeds to Staff review. Staff may refund up to the amount claimed. Both parties are notified.\n\n12.3 Effect on you. A refund decided under the guarantee is deducted from your earnings or recovered from you as provided in section 15, to the extent the claim is attributable to you. Claim volume (order defect rate) affects your standing.\n\n12.4 The guarantee is a Customer remedy. It does not make AfroTransact the seller (except as AfroTransact Retail) or the guarantor of your products to anyone else.\n\n12.5 You agree not to retaliate against Customers who open a claim.\n\n## 13. Reviews\n\n13.1 Customers who received a product may rate and review it. Reviews are published after checks, and some are moderated by Staff before publication. Reviews show the Customer''s first name and last initial.\n\n13.2 You may reply to a review where the Marketplace offers a reply. You may not edit, delete, hide, suppress, buy, trade, incentivise, write or solicit reviews in return for anything or to the exclusion of negative reviews, or write reviews of your own products or competitors'' products. You may report a review for moderation (for example because it contains personal information or is abusive).\n\n13.3 Reply content must be civil, factual and must not disclose personal data of the Customer. We may remove replies that break these rules.\n\n13.4 We may remove reviews and replies that break our policies. We do not remove a review merely because it is negative.\n\n## 14. Payouts and the Hold Window\n\n14.1 Payments are processed by Stripe and settled as described in the Payment Terms. Your earnings from an Order (the item price and your share of shipping, less commission, fees and the payment processing cost allocated to your lines, and with tax handled as in section 16) are credited to your seller balance.\n\n14.2 Hold Window. After a Shipment is Delivered, your earnings for it are held for a hold window. The default is seven (7) days, and Staff may configure it [[LEGAL: confirm that unilateral reconfiguration of the hold window is acceptable and what notice, if any, is required]]. The Hold Window allows time for complaints, claims, delivery problems and disputes to be raised. For services, the hold window starts when the service is completed.\n\n14.3 After the hold, your net earnings are transferred to your connected Stripe account and become available for payout. You request payouts from Seller Center, subject to the minimum amount and frequency shown there (at the time of drafting, a USD 25 minimum and one request a day). Payout requests may be reviewed and approved by our finance team. Payout timing depends on Stripe and your bank.\n\n14.4 No payout before Delivery. We do not transfer earnings for an Order before Delivery (except where the Marketplace expressly provides otherwise).\n\n14.5 Payouts may be paused, delayed or reduced under sections 15, 21 and the Payment Terms.\n\n14.6 You are responsible for your bank details being correct in Stripe. We are not liable for payouts sent to details you provided.\n\n14.7 Unclaimed funds. If we cannot pay you (for example because the account is closed or verification fails), we will try to contact you and may hold the funds as required by law, including unclaimed property rules. [[LEGAL: confirm unclaimed property / escheat handling]]\n\n## 15. Reserves, holds, offsets, chargebacks and disputes\n\n15.1 Holds. We may hold, delay or withhold any amount owed to you, in whole or in part, where we reasonably believe it is needed to cover current or future liabilities, including: an open claim, help request, dispute or chargeback; a suspected breach of these Terms or law; a fraud, sanctions or compliance review; late or undelivered Orders; an account verification issue; a request by Stripe, a card network, a bank or a government authority; or elevated risk indicators in your account.\n\n15.2 Reserves. We may require a reserve, for example a percentage of earnings or a fixed amount held for a stated period, where your risk profile requires it. We will tell you the terms when we set the reserve and will release it when the reason has ended. A reserve is not an interest-bearing deposit and no interest accrues. [[LEGAL: confirm reserve structure, whether funds are held in a segregated account, and money-transmission positioning]]\n\n15.3 Offset and recovery. We may deduct, set off or recover from any amount owed to you, from a reserve, from your card or bank on file, or from your connected account through debits Stripe permits: refunds and claims attributable to you; chargebacks, dispute fees and fines; shipping label costs and handling; plan fees; fees for delivery trips wasted by your fault; the cost of Customer credit issued because of your breach; amounts you owe under any other agreement with us; and any overpayment.\n\n15.4 Chargebacks. A chargeback is a Customer''s bank reversing a payment. Where a chargeback relates to your Order we will allocate it to you, together with fees and fines. We may respond to the chargeback on your behalf or ask for your evidence by the deadline given. You must provide evidence on time. The card network or bank decides. If the chargeback is lost, the amount is recovered from you.\n\n15.5 Negative balances. If your balance is negative, you owe the amount to us (and, to the extent the Stripe agreements provide, to Stripe). We may recover it as in 15.3, stop payouts until it is cleared and charge your payment method. We may charge reasonable collection costs where permitted by law.\n\n15.6 Your disputes with us. If you dispute a hold, offset or charge, contact support with your evidence. We will review in good faith. You must continue to perform your obligations while a dispute is open.\n\n15.7 Records. Our ledger is the record of what you are owed; it is available to you as a statement.\n\n## 16. Taxes\n\n16.1 Sales tax. AfroTransact calculates, collects and remits sales tax on eligible Marketplace sales as marketplace facilitator where the law of the relevant jurisdiction requires or permits it [[LEGAL: tax registration status and the states in which AfroTransact is registered and collecting; confirm marketplace-facilitator positions per state]]. The tax is shown separately at checkout. You must not collect the same sales tax a second time on Orders on the Marketplace. We calculate tax using rates and rules maintained by AfroTransact, based on your product''s tax classification and the delivery address. You must classify your products accurately. We are not liable for the consequences of inaccurate classification you provide.\n\n16.2 Where tax is not collected by us (for example where the law does not treat us as the collecting party, or for off-platform sales), you remain responsible for it.\n\n16.3 Statements. We show a tax statement per seller. Statements are provided for information; they are not tax advice.\n\n16.4 Your taxes. You remain responsible for all your own taxes, including income tax, self-employment tax, franchise or gross receipts taxes, business licences, and any sales or use tax on your own purchases and on sales we do not collect. You are responsible for your own registration, filings and records. We do not give tax advice.\n\n16.5 Tax information. You must give us accurate tax identification and forms (such as a W-9) and update them. We may withhold payouts, or withhold tax where required (including backup withholding), if you do not. We report your sales and payments to tax authorities as required, for example on Form 1099-K or 1099-NEC or equivalent [[LEGAL: confirm information reporting obligations and thresholds]].\n\n16.6 Tax on our fees. Our fees may be subject to tax. If so, we will add it and you will pay it.\n\n16.7 Refunds and tax. When an Order is refunded, associated tax is reversed according to our processes.\n\n16.8 Compliance. You are responsible for complying with all laws that apply to your business, including consumer protection, product safety, food safety, labelling, advertising, import and export, customs, privacy, and anti-corruption laws.\n\n## 17. Prohibited items and conduct\n\n17.1 You must not list, sell or ship anything that is prohibited by law or by our Prohibited & Restricted Items policy (which includes weapons, illegal drugs, counterfeit goods, protected wildlife products, recalled items, unapproved medicines and supplements making disease claims). Restricted items need our written approval and the licences required.\n\n17.2 You must not use the Marketplace for any activity listed as prohibited or restricted by Stripe in its list of prohibited and restricted businesses, which is incorporated into these Terms by reference [[LEGAL: confirm incorporation by reference of Stripe''s list and country-specific lists]]. You must not use the Marketplace for money transmission, gift cards or stored value not issued by us, escrow, crowdfunding, or financial or legal services not approved by us.\n\n17.3 You must not: commit fraud; launder money; evade sanctions; manipulate prices, ratings, search ranking or reviews; engage in unfair or deceptive practices; infringe rights of others; send spam; harass or discriminate; attempt to bypass our systems; scrape or abuse the Marketplace; or transmit malware.\n\n17.4 We may report suspected illegal activity to authorities.\n\n## 18. Intellectual property\n\n18.1 You keep ownership of your content (product text, images, brand names and marks). You grant AfroTransact a worldwide, non-exclusive, royalty-free, sublicensable licence to host, reproduce, adapt (for example to resize, translate or format), display, and distribute your content on the Marketplace, in search, in marketing, in emails and messages, in our apps and with our partners, in connection with operating and promoting the Marketplace and your products. The licence continues for a reasonable period after removal as needed for orders, records and backups.\n\n18.2 You warrant that you own or have the licence to your content and that it does not infringe or violate others'' rights or the law.\n\n18.3 Our brand and software belong to us. You may not use AfroTransact marks without permission, except to say that you sell on AfroTransact in line with our brand guidelines.\n\n18.4 IP complaints are handled under the Intellectual Property policy. We may remove content, restrict accounts and terminate repeat infringers.\n\n18.5 If you give feedback we may use it freely.\n\n## 19. Data protection\n\n19.1 Roles. For personal data of Customers that you receive to fulfil an Order (such as name, delivery address, phone and email, and order content), you are an independent controller (or \"business\" under U.S. privacy laws) for your own purposes of fulfilling that Order and complying with your own legal obligations. AfroTransact is an independent controller for the data it collects for operating the Marketplace, payments, fraud prevention, support and other purposes in our Privacy Notice. Where AfroTransact processes personal data on your behalf (for example hosting your catalogue or staff data), the Data Processing Terms apply.\n\n19.2 Your obligations. You must use Customer data only to fulfil the Order, provide support for it and comply with law; not sell or share it, or use it for marketing or profiling; keep it secure; not retain it longer than needed; delete it as the Data Processing Terms require; give Customers their privacy rights directly where you are their controller; and tell us promptly if you suffer a security incident affecting Customer data.\n\n19.3 Customer messages are sent via the Marketplace. You may not add Customers to your own marketing lists without their separate, lawful opt-in outside of order data.\n\n19.4 Data about you. We process the data you give us as described in the Privacy Notice and the Sub-processors list, including to verify identity, assess risk, pay you and comply with law. We may share your business details and information about disputes with Stripe, card networks, banks, law enforcement and regulators.\n\n19.5 Messaging consent. You must not use Marketplace contact details to send texts or WhatsApp messages unless the Customer has consented to receive them from you under applicable law.\n\n## 20. Warranties and your responsibility for your products\n\n20.1 You warrant that: the information you give us is true; you have the right to sell what you list; your products are safe, lawful, fit for the stated purpose, and as described; you comply with all applicable laws and these Terms; you are not subject to sanctions and are not owned or controlled by sanctioned persons; and you hold and keep valid all licences and insurance required.\n\n20.2 You are responsible for product liability and for the quality, safety, legality, description and delivery of your products. You must carry the insurance that is appropriate for your business [[LEGAL: specify any minimum product liability insurance required, by category]].\n\n20.3 We make no warranty about the volume of sales, the availability of the Marketplace or the conduct of Customers. The Marketplace is provided as available.\n\n## 21. Suspension, restriction and termination\n\n21.1 By us. We may limit, hide listings, pause payouts, suspend or terminate your account, with or without notice, if: you breach these Terms or a policy; we reasonably suspect fraud, illegality, counterfeiting, prohibited items or abuse; your performance falls below standard; Stripe or another provider restricts or closes your account or requires us to act; you fail verification or fail to keep information current; your licences or insurance lapse; there are excessive claims, chargebacks or complaints; we must comply with law, a court or regulator; you create risk to Customers, to us or to others; or you are inactive. We will tell you the reason where we can and where the law permits, and in cases of performance-based action, will give notice and an opportunity to respond or appeal before termination, except where urgent action is needed [[LEGAL: confirm notice and appeal commitments; these are derived from the existing seeded terms]].\n\n21.2 By you. You may close your account at any time, after you have fulfilled open Orders, paid what you owe, and completed any open claims. Plan fees already paid are not refunded unless we say so or the law requires.\n\n21.3 Effect. On termination or suspension: your listings are removed; open Orders must be fulfilled or are cancelled by us; payouts of remaining amounts are made after the Hold Window and after we have deducted what you owe and any reserve we need for expected claims and chargebacks (we may hold a reserve for up to [[LEGAL: reserve retention period after termination, for example 90 to 180 days]]); and sections that by nature continue will survive, including 6, 11 to 16, 18 to 20 and 22 to 27.\n\n21.4 Suspension and termination do not relieve you of responsibility for Orders you accepted or amounts you owe.\n\n## 22. Indemnity\n\n22.1 You will defend, indemnify and hold harmless AfroTransact, its affiliates, officers, employees and agents from claims, losses, liabilities, penalties, damages, costs and expenses (including reasonable legal fees) arising out of or related to: your products, listings and content; your breach of these Terms, the Payment Terms or the law; your taxes; Customer or third-party claims relating to your products, delivery or conduct; product liability, recalls and food safety; infringement claims about your content; your use of Customer data; and your negligence or wilful misconduct.\n\n22.2 We will tell you promptly of a claim and cooperate at your cost. You may not settle a claim that imposes an obligation on us or admits fault without our consent.\n\n## 23. Disclaimers and limitation of liability\n\n23.1 [[LEGAL: disclaimers drafted to the governing law; confirm \"as is\" language and conspicuous formatting required by law]] The Marketplace and our services are provided \"as is\" and \"as available\". To the fullest extent permitted by law we disclaim all implied warranties, including merchantability, fitness for a particular purpose and non-infringement.\n\n23.2 To the fullest extent permitted by law, AfroTransact and its affiliates will not be liable to you for indirect, incidental, special, consequential, exemplary or punitive damages, or for lost profits, revenue, goodwill or data, even if advised of the possibility.\n\n23.3 AfroTransact''s total liability for all claims relating to these Terms and the Marketplace will not exceed [[LEGAL: liability cap, for example the fees you paid us in the 12 months before the claim or a fixed amount]].\n\n23.4 Nothing excludes liability that cannot be excluded by law, including for fraud or wilful misconduct. [[LEGAL: carve-outs and state-specific rules]]\n\n23.5 We are not responsible for failures of Stripe, carriers, communications providers, hosting providers or other third parties beyond our reasonable control, or for events outside our control.\n\n## 24. Disputes with AfroTransact, governing law and venue\n\n24.1 [[LEGAL: governing law]] governs these Terms.\n\n24.2 [[LEGAL: exclusive venue / courts; or arbitration provision, class-action waiver, small-claims carve-out, opt-out period and arbitration provider]]\n\n24.3 Before starting a formal proceeding you will send us written notice of the dispute at {legal} and give us thirty (30) days to resolve it [[LEGAL: confirm informal resolution period]].\n\n## 25. Changes to these Terms\n\n25.1 We may change these Terms, and the policies and documents they incorporate, by posting the new version in Seller Center or notifying you. For material changes we give at least thirty (30) days'' notice [[LEGAL: confirm notice period]], except where a shorter period is required by law, by Stripe or by a regulator, or where the change corrects an error or benefits you.\n\n25.2 If you keep selling after the effective date, you accept the new version. If you do not accept, you may close your account before the effective date.\n\n25.3 Each version is archived with its effective date and a summary of changes. A certified copy of earlier versions is available on request at {legal}.\n\n## 26. Records and cooperation\n\n26.1 Keep your Order, tax, licence, insurance, sourcing and delivery records for at least the longer of seven (7) years and the period required by law [[LEGAL: confirm retention period; the Privacy Notice states 7 years for order records]]. Provide records to us on request within a reasonable time for audits, tax, compliance, disputes and regulator requests.\n\n26.2 You will cooperate with our investigations and with any regulator or law-enforcement request that concerns your activity.\n\n## 27. General\n\n27.1 Independent contractors. You are an independent business. Nothing creates a partnership, agency, franchise, employment or joint venture. You decide how to run your business, subject to these Terms.\n\n27.2 Assignment. You may not assign these Terms without our consent. We may assign them to an affiliate or a successor.\n\n27.3 Notices. We may notify you in Seller Center, by email or by text message to your contact details. You must keep them current. Send notices to us at {legal} and at {address}.\n\n27.4 Entire agreement and severability. These Terms and the documents they incorporate are the whole agreement on their subject. If a provision is unenforceable the rest continues.\n\n27.5 No waiver. Not enforcing a right is not a waiver.\n\n27.6 Force majeure. Neither party is liable for delay or failure caused by events beyond its reasonable control, except for payment obligations.\n\n27.7 Language. The English version governs.\n\n27.8 Export, sanctions and anti-corruption. You will comply with applicable sanctions, export-control and anti-corruption laws.\n\n27.9 Third-party beneficiaries. Stripe may enforce the parts of these Terms that relate to its services as stated in the Payment Terms. Otherwise there are no third-party beneficiaries.\n\n27.10 Contact. Questions go to {legal}. Our address is {address}."}'::jsonb, 'Full rewrite of the seeded Seller & Provider Terms 2.4. Splits service-provider terms, payment terms, fees summary, data-processing terms and the sub-processor list into their own documents; adds two-step onboarding review, billing waterfall, delivery proof, the support and evidence dispute process, reserves and offsets, tax roles and data-protection roles.', now(), 'legal-loader', now(), null
 where not exists (select 1 from legal_version where doc_type = 'seller-terms' and (major, minor, patch) >= (1, 0, 0));
update legal_version set effective_date = '2026-12-01'::date, draft_banner = true,
       bodies = '{"en": "# Seller Terms\n\n> DRAFT. This document was prepared with AI assistance and has not been reviewed by qualified counsel. It must be reviewed and approved by counsel before it is published or relied on. Text in double square brackets marked LEGAL is an open decision.\n\n## 1. Agreement and definitions\n\n1.1 These Seller Terms (the \"Terms\") are a contract between you (the business or individual that applies for or holds a seller account, \"Seller\", \"you\") and [[LEGAL: contracting entity name, form, state of organisation and registered address for AfroTransact]] (\"AfroTransact\", \"we\", \"us\").\n\n1.2 The following documents form part of the agreement and are incorporated by reference. If they conflict, the order of priority is: the Payment Terms (for anything about money movement, Stripe, payouts, holds and negative balances); the Data Processing Terms (for personal data); these Terms; the Seller Fees Summary; then any policy page we link to.\n- Payment Terms for Sellers and Providers (\"Payment Terms\")\n- Data Processing Terms (\"Data Processing Terms\")\n- Seller Fees Summary and the current fee schedule shown in Seller Center\n- Sub-processors list\n- Prohibited & Restricted Items policy, Intellectual Property policy and Community Guidelines\n- Returns & Refunds policy (as it applies to customers) and the Privacy Notice\n\n1.3 Capitalised terms: \"Marketplace\" means AfroTransact''s websites, apps and APIs. \"Seller Center\" is your back office. \"Order\" is a customer purchase of products. \"Shipment\" is the part of an Order fulfilled from one location by one fulfilment method. \"Delivered\" means the time a Shipment is recorded as handed over under section 9. \"Hold Window\" means the settlement hold in section 14. \"Customer\" is a shopper. \"AfroTransact Retail\" is AfroTransact''s own first-party retail business, which sells on the Marketplace as a seller of record. \"Staff\" means authorised AfroTransact personnel.\n\n1.4 If you offer services or bookings, the Service Provider Terms also apply to that part of your activity. If you do both, both documents apply and the Service Provider Terms control for services.\n\n1.5 By submitting an application, accepting these Terms in Seller Center or listing a product, you agree to them. If you accept for a business, you confirm that you are authorised to bind it.\n\n## 2. Eligibility and onboarding review\n\n2.1 To sell you must: be at least 18 and have legal capacity; be a legal entity or an individual (sole proprietor) able to enter binding contracts; operate in a market that is live on the Marketplace (we will tell you which); hold every licence, permit, registration and insurance required for what you sell (for example food permits, cosmetic labelling compliance and any state or local licences); and not be subject to sanctions or located in or ordinarily resident in an embargoed jurisdiction.\n\n2.2 Application. You complete a resumable application covering business details, owners and persons with significant control, tax information, payout and identity verification, plan choice, and catalogue compliance information (such as permits for food or perishables). All information must be accurate, complete and kept up to date. We may ask for documents and for more information at any time.\n\n2.3 Two-step review. Every application is reviewed in two separate steps by two different members of Staff: a first review of documents and checks, and a final approval by a second person. Either step may approve, request more information, flag the application, send it back or reject it. We record the reason for each decision. We are not required to approve any application and may decline for any lawful reason, including risk, prohibited categories, sanctions or screening results, duplicate accounts, reputation or incomplete information.\n\n2.4 Identity and business verification is performed by Stripe, our payment processor, as described in the Payment Terms. You must pass Stripe''s verification and keep your connected account in good standing. If Stripe restricts your account, we may hide your listings and pause payouts under section 21.\n\n2.5 Approval is for the offering we approve (for example products, services or both) and the categories we approve. You may not sell in a restricted category without our specific approval.\n\n2.6 Ongoing eligibility. You must tell us promptly of any change to ownership, control, legal name, address, licences, permits, insurance, tax status or anything else that could affect these answers. Expired or revoked licences and permits may result in listings being hidden.\n\n## 3. Accounts, roles and security\n\n3.1 Your account has an owner and may have members with roles such as admin, catalogue, finance, fulfilment, support and staff. The owner is responsible for who is invited, for the permissions they hold, and for removing people who leave.\n\n3.2 You are responsible for everything done through your account and by your members, agents and integrations. Keep credentials secret, use the multi-factor options we offer and tell us immediately of any suspected compromise.\n\n3.3 One person may hold customer, seller and provider roles on one sign-in. Your seller activity is separate from your activity as a shopper, and you may not use a seller account to buy your own products, to inflate sales, reviews or ratings, or to circumvent a restriction.\n\n3.4 Only owners and finance members may change payout or plan settings and open Stripe account management. You must keep at least one active owner at all times.\n\n## 4. Listings and product rules\n\n4.1 You must only list products you are lawfully entitled to sell and have in stock or can fulfil by the stated date. A listing must be accurate, complete and not misleading. It must include the true condition, quantity, ingredients and allergens where relevant, country of origin where required, size or weight, and any warnings.\n\n4.2 Food, supplements, cosmetics and other regulated goods must comply with all laws that apply to the sale and shipment of that product, including labelling, registration, permits, shelf life, cold-chain and import rules. Perishables must be shipped or delivered in a way that keeps them safe. You are responsible for recalls and must tell us immediately if a product you sold is recalled or unsafe.\n\n4.3 Listing content you or a tool prepare (including text generated with our AI listing tools) remains your responsibility. You must review it before publishing.\n\n4.4 Listings are moderated. We may review, hold, edit the presentation of, hide or remove a listing at any time, including automatically, for policy, safety, legal, quality or risk reasons. We may place listings in a moderation queue before they go live.\n\n4.5 Product identity. We match your listing to the catalogue where possible. We may merge or de-duplicate listings, assign identifiers, and apply category and tax classification. You must answer tax-related listing questions accurately.\n\n4.6 You may not: list a prohibited item; list the same product in a misleading way to avoid detection; use another seller''s content or images without permission; use competitor names or counterfeit marks; solicit customers to buy or pay outside the Marketplace; ask Customers for off-platform contact or payment; or collect Customer information for any purpose other than fulfilling the Order and your legal obligations.\n\n4.7 Inventory. If you lose inventory or cannot fulfil, you must update or withdraw the listing promptly. Repeated stock-outs after the order is placed are counted as cancellations under section 10.\n\n## 5. Pricing\n\n5.1 You set your prices in US dollars unless we enable other currencies. Prices must be the total price of the product before taxes and any delivery fee that we show separately. Customer-facing prices must be honest and must not be inflated on the Marketplace to cover fees in a way our policies prohibit.\n\n5.2 We may display discounts, coupons, credits, membership benefits, or promotions funded by us or by you. Promotions you fund reduce your earnings only as you agreed when you created the promotion. Promotions we fund are not deducted from your earnings unless we told you so in the promotion terms.\n\n5.3 We calculate and show shipping, delivery fees and taxes at checkout according to rules that we configure (see section 8 and section 16). You may not add hidden surcharges.\n\n5.4 A price error is your responsibility. We may cancel or correct an Order where the displayed price was clearly wrong, subject to our policies and applicable law.\n\n## 6. Fees, commission and plan fees\n\n6.1 Types of fees. You pay AfroTransact (a) a plan fee for your chosen plan, if any; (b) a commission, as a percentage of each product sale; (c) for services only, a connection fee (see the Service Provider Terms); (d) fees for optional or required services such as fulfilment by AfroTransact, shipping labels bought through us, handling, and delivery fees where the delivery fee rule assigns them to the seller; and (e) the Stripe processing and payout fees that Stripe charges, which are borne as described in the Payment Terms.\n\n6.2 Schedules are configured, not fixed in these Terms. The rates and amounts are set in fee schedules and plan catalogues that AfroTransact Staff configure. They may depend on your plan, the product category, rates we negotiate with you in writing, and the delivery method. The Seller Fees Summary explains how this works. The rate that applies to a sale is recorded when the order is placed and does not change for that order if we later change the schedule.\n\n6.3 Where to see them. The fee schedule that applies to you, your plan price and any negotiated rate and its end date are shown in Seller Center. A statement shows commission and fees per order line.\n\n6.4 Changes. We may change fees, plan prices and schedules by giving at least thirty (30) days'' notice [[LEGAL: confirm minimum notice period for fee changes and whether shorter notice is acceptable for changes that benefit sellers or are required by law or by Stripe]]. A change takes effect for new orders after the notice period. Existing plan subscribers stay on their plan version until their next renewal unless they agree to migrate sooner. If you do not agree to a change you may stop selling and close your account before it takes effect.\n\n6.5 Plan trials, free periods and rewards. We may offer trials, free months, credits, rewards, or charge conditions (for example, a plan that is free until certain sales or listing thresholds are reached). These are subject to their stated conditions, are decided by the rules stated when offered, are given once per seller unless stated otherwise, and may be withdrawn for abuse or misrepresentation.\n\n6.6 AfroTransact Retail does not pay commission or plan fees to AfroTransact. This does not affect your fees.\n\n6.7 Refunds and fee treatment. On a refund, the commission is returned to you in the proportion the schedule provides for. Delivery fees, label costs, processing costs and other fees may be kept or returned depending on who was at fault, as described in the Seller Fees Summary.\n\n## 7. Billing waterfall for plan fees\n\n7.1 Your plan fee is billed through our billing provider on the cycle you choose (monthly, quarterly or annual, as offered).\n\n7.2 Order of settlement. When an invoice for a plan fee falls due, it is satisfied in this order:\n1. first, by any billing credit, reward, coupon, trial or free-period balance on your account;\n2. second, if you have opted in to \"pay your plan from your sales\" and the feature is switched on for your account, from your pending earnings that are eligible (delivered, undisputed and not yet transferred); and\n3. third, by charging your card or bank account on file for any remaining balance.\n\n7.3 If you do not opt in, steps 1 and 3 apply. You may change your choice in Seller Center; a change applies from the next invoice, not to an invoice already created. We may switch the earnings option off at any time, in which case invoices are charged to your card.\n\n7.4 Earnings withheld for a plan fee are not held for any other reason. If the invoice is voided, credited or refunded, the withheld amount is released and paid to you with a later transfer.\n\n7.5 If a charge fails we may retry, suspend plan benefits or features, downgrade you to a plan without those features, or suspend your account after notice as stated in section 21.\n\n7.6 Invoices and receipts are available in Seller Center.\n\n## 8. Orders and fulfilment obligations\n\n8.1 When a Customer places an Order that includes your products, a contract of sale is formed between you and the Customer (or, for AfroTransact Retail, between AfroTransact Retail and the Customer). You are the seller of record. AfroTransact is not a party to your sale contract except where it sells as AfroTransact Retail. We provide the Marketplace and payment and related services.\n\n8.2 You must accept, pack and hand over each Order by the ship-by or ready-by time shown in Seller Center. You must pack items to prevent damage, keep perishables safe, and include the correct goods in the correct quantity.\n\n8.3 Fulfilment methods. Depending on your account and the listing, an Order may be fulfilled by:\n- the seller''s own shipping through a carrier you choose or through carrier labels bought via the Marketplace;\n- carrier shipping using labels bought through AfroTransact and charged to you at the rate shown, including any handling charge;\n- fulfilment by AfroTransact (stock you have sent to AfroTransact), under the applicable inbound and fulfilment terms and fees;\n- a delivery run, where AfroTransact collects from you or delivers from its own inventory for a configured place and date, with fees as configured;\n- pickup by the Customer from your location or a pickup point; or\n- delivery by you or your own courier.\n\n8.4 Delivery runs. For runs, AfroTransact or its drivers may collect your goods at a time we tell you. A run may be cancelled or extended by Staff, for example if its minimum order count is not met. When a run is cancelled, all Orders on it are cancelled and refunded in full; we will tell you. You are not owed lost sales for a cancelled run [[LEGAL: confirm that seller compensation is excluded]]. Delivery fees on runs are configured by Staff and shown at checkout; the fee rule applied is recorded on the Order.\n\n8.5 Performance. We measure late shipment rate, seller cancellation rate, order defect rate and response times over a rolling period against targets we publish. Below-target accounts may be limited (for example in search or buy-box placement), suspended or terminated under section 21.\n\n8.6 Customer communications. Use Marketplace messaging for Order communications. You must respond to support and claim requests within the time shown. You may not market to Customers or direct them off-platform.\n\n8.7 Packaging and documents. Do not include advertising or off-platform solicitations. Use the packing slip we provide where required.\n\n8.8 You are responsible for loss and damage until Delivery except where the Marketplace expressly provides otherwise for AfroTransact-handled shipments.\n\n## 9. Shipping, delivery proof and codes\n\n9.1 Tracking. Carrier shipments must be shipped with valid tracking, and tracking must show delivery to the address on the Order. Mark shipments shipped only when handed to the carrier.\n\n9.2 Delivery is recorded when: the carrier reports delivery; an AfroTransact driver completes delivery with proof (a photo and handover method, and for hand-to-customer and perishable deliveries a code or signature as required); the Customer collects a pickup order and the Customer''s four-digit handover code is entered or checked; or Staff confirm delivery with a recorded reason.\n\n9.3 Handover codes. Each pickup order and each hand-to-customer or perishable driver delivery has a code the Customer sees on their order. For pickup and seller-delivered orders you must ask for the code and must not hand over without it unless Staff instruct otherwise. You must never ask Customers to share the code before handover, send it to others or write it on a package.\n\n9.4 Proof. Keep evidence of dispatch and delivery (receipts, tracking, photos) for the retention period in section 26. Where AfroTransact collects proof, it is shown to you, the Customer and Staff on the order.\n\n9.5 Seller delivery. If you deliver yourself or through your own courier you are responsible for the courier, for safe handling and for obtaining proof as the Marketplace requires. You warrant that you are authorised and insured to do so.\n\n9.6 Delivery estimates and customers'' preferences (such as safe place) must be followed where reasonable. Safe-place delivery is at the risk stated in the order.\n\n9.7 Risk of loss for carrier and seller deliveries passes to the Customer on Delivery. If a shipment is not delivered, is damaged or is wrong, the dispute process in section 11 and the claims process in section 12 apply.\n\n## 10. Cancellations\n\n10.1 Customer cancellations. Customers may cancel before packing and within the cancellation window shown at checkout. You must not pack or ship an Order that has been cancelled. An authorisation not yet captured is released; a captured payment is refunded.\n\n10.2 Seller cancellations. You may cancel only for a legitimate reason (for example stock error or a safety issue) and must do so as soon as you know. Cancellations by you or by the system because you did not ship count against your performance and may be subject to fees, including recovery of costs we incurred such as a wasted trip or label.\n\n10.3 Staff cancellations. Staff may cancel any Order for fraud, risk, legal or operational reasons.\n\n10.4 Cancellation after pickup. If an Order is cancelled after a delivery trip or label cost is incurred, the cost may be allocated to the party at fault, as set out in the Seller Fees Summary.\n\n## 11. No self-service returns for delivered orders; support and evidence disputes\n\n11.1 AfroTransact does not offer Customers a self-service return flow for Delivered Orders. A Customer who has a problem with a Delivered Order (for example not received, damaged, wrong item, not as described, or quality problem) opens a help request with a description and photo or other evidence. Our Staff decide the outcome.\n\n11.2 Seller role. We may notify you and ask you to respond with your account and evidence within the response window shown on the request. If you do not respond in time, Staff may decide without your response.\n\n11.3 Staff decide. Staff may approve a full or partial refund, a replacement, a credit, no remedy, or another outcome, based on the evidence, the listing, delivery proof, the policies and applicable law. The decision and reasons are recorded and shown to the parties. Staff may ask for more evidence and may escalate. Decisions are made at Staff discretion acting reasonably and are final within the Marketplace, subject to the appeal in 11.7.\n\n11.4 Seller liability. If the outcome is a refund and the problem is attributable to you (for example wrong, damaged, missing or not-as-described goods, late or non-delivery, or breach of these Terms), the refund is charged to you and recovered under section 15. Where the problem is attributable to a carrier or to AfroTransact, we may bear it. A refund may be returned to the Customer to the original payment method or, where Staff decide inside a support resolution, as AfroTransact credit; the way a refund is returned to the Customer does not change what you are charged, unless we tell you otherwise.\n\n11.5 Returned goods. Staff may instruct that goods be returned to you, held, or disposed of (for example perishables). We are not required to require a return before refunding. You must not demand that Customers return goods outside the Marketplace.\n\n11.6 Evidence. You must provide true and complete evidence. Fabricated or misleading evidence is grounds for termination and recovery of losses.\n\n11.7 Appeal. You may ask for a review of a decision through support within the period stated in the decision, giving new evidence. A second Staff member will review. [[LEGAL: confirm whether the appeal process should be offered at all and its time limit]]\n\n11.8 Nothing in this section limits rights a Customer has under consumer law.\n\n## 12. Claims and the A-to-z style guarantee\n\n12.1 AfroTransact provides a Customer guarantee for Orders placed on the Marketplace when something goes wrong (for example a product is not received, is damaged, or is not what was described). Claims are opened by Customers with evidence and decided by Staff.\n\n12.2 Process. A claim moves through: awaiting your response; under review; and then resolved with a refund or denial, or escalated. A claim has response and decision targets. If you do not respond in time, the claim proceeds to Staff review. Staff may refund up to the amount claimed. Both parties are notified.\n\n12.3 Effect on you. A refund decided under the guarantee is deducted from your earnings or recovered from you as provided in section 15, to the extent the claim is attributable to you. Claim volume (order defect rate) affects your standing.\n\n12.4 The guarantee is a Customer remedy. It does not make AfroTransact the seller (except as AfroTransact Retail) or the guarantor of your products to anyone else.\n\n12.5 You agree not to retaliate against Customers who open a claim.\n\n## 13. Reviews\n\n13.1 Customers who received a product may rate and review it. Reviews are published after checks, and some are moderated by Staff before publication. Reviews show the Customer''s first name and last initial.\n\n13.2 You may reply to a review where the Marketplace offers a reply. You may not edit, delete, hide, suppress, buy, trade, incentivise, write or solicit reviews in return for anything or to the exclusion of negative reviews, or write reviews of your own products or competitors'' products. You may report a review for moderation (for example because it contains personal information or is abusive).\n\n13.3 Reply content must be civil, factual and must not disclose personal data of the Customer. We may remove replies that break these rules.\n\n13.4 We may remove reviews and replies that break our policies. We do not remove a review merely because it is negative.\n\n## 14. Payouts and the Hold Window\n\n14.1 Payments are processed by Stripe and settled as described in the Payment Terms. Your earnings from an Order (the item price and your share of shipping, less commission, fees and the payment processing cost allocated to your lines, and with tax handled as in section 16) are credited to your seller balance.\n\n14.2 Hold Window. After a Shipment is Delivered, your earnings for it are held for a hold window. The default is seven (7) days, and Staff may configure it [[LEGAL: confirm that unilateral reconfiguration of the hold window is acceptable and what notice, if any, is required]]. The Hold Window allows time for complaints, claims, delivery problems and disputes to be raised. For services, the hold window starts when the service is completed.\n\n14.3 After the hold, your net earnings are transferred to your connected Stripe account and become available for payout. You request payouts from Seller Center, subject to the minimum amount and frequency shown there (at the time of drafting, a USD 25 minimum and one request a day). Payout requests may be reviewed and approved by our finance team. Payout timing depends on Stripe and your bank.\n\n14.4 No payout before Delivery. We do not transfer earnings for an Order before Delivery (except where the Marketplace expressly provides otherwise).\n\n14.5 Payouts may be paused, delayed or reduced under sections 15, 21 and the Payment Terms.\n\n14.6 You are responsible for your bank details being correct in Stripe. We are not liable for payouts sent to details you provided.\n\n14.7 Unclaimed funds. If we cannot pay you (for example because the account is closed or verification fails), we will try to contact you and may hold the funds as required by law, including unclaimed property rules. [[LEGAL: confirm unclaimed property / escheat handling]]\n\n## 15. Reserves, holds, offsets, chargebacks and disputes\n\n15.1 Holds. We may hold, delay or withhold any amount owed to you, in whole or in part, where we reasonably believe it is needed to cover current or future liabilities, including: an open claim, help request, dispute or chargeback; a suspected breach of these Terms or law; a fraud, sanctions or compliance review; late or undelivered Orders; an account verification issue; a request by Stripe, a card network, a bank or a government authority; or elevated risk indicators in your account.\n\n15.2 Reserves. We may require a reserve, for example a percentage of earnings or a fixed amount held for a stated period, where your risk profile requires it. We will tell you the terms when we set the reserve and will release it when the reason has ended. A reserve is not an interest-bearing deposit and no interest accrues. [[LEGAL: confirm reserve structure, whether funds are held in a segregated account, and money-transmission positioning]]\n\n15.3 Offset and recovery. We may deduct, set off or recover from any amount owed to you, from a reserve, from your card or bank on file, or from your connected account through debits Stripe permits: refunds and claims attributable to you; chargebacks, dispute fees and fines; shipping label costs and handling; plan fees; fees for delivery trips wasted by your fault; the cost of Customer credit issued because of your breach; amounts you owe under any other agreement with us; and any overpayment.\n\n15.4 Chargebacks. A chargeback is a Customer''s bank reversing a payment. Where a chargeback relates to your Order we will allocate it to you, together with fees and fines. We may respond to the chargeback on your behalf or ask for your evidence by the deadline given. You must provide evidence on time. The card network or bank decides. If the chargeback is lost, the amount is recovered from you.\n\n15.5 Negative balances. If your balance is negative, you owe the amount to us (and, to the extent the Stripe agreements provide, to Stripe). We may recover it as in 15.3, stop payouts until it is cleared and charge your payment method. We may charge reasonable collection costs where permitted by law.\n\n15.6 Your disputes with us. If you dispute a hold, offset or charge, contact support with your evidence. We will review in good faith. You must continue to perform your obligations while a dispute is open.\n\n15.7 Records. Our ledger is the record of what you are owed; it is available to you as a statement.\n\n## 16. Taxes\n\n16.1 Sales tax. AfroTransact calculates, collects and remits sales tax on eligible Marketplace sales as marketplace facilitator where the law of the relevant jurisdiction requires or permits it [[LEGAL: tax registration status and the states in which AfroTransact is registered and collecting; confirm marketplace-facilitator positions per state]]. The tax is shown separately at checkout. You must not collect the same sales tax a second time on Orders on the Marketplace. We calculate tax using rates and rules maintained by AfroTransact, based on your product''s tax classification and the delivery address. You must classify your products accurately. We are not liable for the consequences of inaccurate classification you provide.\n\n16.2 Where tax is not collected by us (for example where the law does not treat us as the collecting party, or for off-platform sales), you remain responsible for it.\n\n16.3 Statements. We show a tax statement per seller. Statements are provided for information; they are not tax advice.\n\n16.4 Your taxes. You remain responsible for all your own taxes, including income tax, self-employment tax, franchise or gross receipts taxes, business licences, and any sales or use tax on your own purchases and on sales we do not collect. You are responsible for your own registration, filings and records. We do not give tax advice.\n\n16.5 Tax information. You must give us accurate tax identification and forms (such as a W-9) and update them. We may withhold payouts, or withhold tax where required (including backup withholding), if you do not. We report your sales and payments to tax authorities as required, for example on Form 1099-K or 1099-NEC or equivalent [[LEGAL: confirm information reporting obligations and thresholds]].\n\n16.6 Tax on our fees. Our fees may be subject to tax. If so, we will add it and you will pay it.\n\n16.7 Refunds and tax. When an Order is refunded, associated tax is reversed according to our processes.\n\n16.8 Compliance. You are responsible for complying with all laws that apply to your business, including consumer protection, product safety, food safety, labelling, advertising, import and export, customs, privacy, and anti-corruption laws.\n\n## 17. Prohibited items and conduct\n\n17.1 You must not list, sell or ship anything that is prohibited by law or by our Prohibited & Restricted Items policy (which includes weapons, illegal drugs, counterfeit goods, protected wildlife products, recalled items, unapproved medicines and supplements making disease claims). Restricted items need our written approval and the licences required.\n\n17.2 You must not use the Marketplace for any activity listed as prohibited or restricted by Stripe in its list of prohibited and restricted businesses, which is incorporated into these Terms by reference [[LEGAL: confirm incorporation by reference of Stripe''s list and country-specific lists]]. You must not use the Marketplace for money transmission, gift cards or stored value not issued by us, escrow, crowdfunding, or financial or legal services not approved by us.\n\n17.3 You must not: commit fraud; launder money; evade sanctions; manipulate prices, ratings, search ranking or reviews; engage in unfair or deceptive practices; infringe rights of others; send spam; harass or discriminate; attempt to bypass our systems; scrape or abuse the Marketplace; or transmit malware.\n\n17.4 We may report suspected illegal activity to authorities.\n\n## 18. Intellectual property\n\n18.1 You keep ownership of your content (product text, images, brand names and marks). You grant AfroTransact a worldwide, non-exclusive, royalty-free, sublicensable licence to host, reproduce, adapt (for example to resize, translate or format), display, and distribute your content on the Marketplace, in search, in marketing, in emails and messages, in our apps and with our partners, in connection with operating and promoting the Marketplace and your products. The licence continues for a reasonable period after removal as needed for orders, records and backups.\n\n18.2 You warrant that you own or have the licence to your content and that it does not infringe or violate others'' rights or the law.\n\n18.3 Our brand and software belong to us. You may not use AfroTransact marks without permission, except to say that you sell on AfroTransact in line with our brand guidelines.\n\n18.4 IP complaints are handled under the Intellectual Property policy. We may remove content, restrict accounts and terminate repeat infringers.\n\n18.5 If you give feedback we may use it freely.\n\n## 19. Data protection\n\n19.1 Roles. For personal data of Customers that you receive to fulfil an Order (such as name, delivery address, phone and email, and order content), you are an independent controller (or \"business\" under U.S. privacy laws) for your own purposes of fulfilling that Order and complying with your own legal obligations. AfroTransact is an independent controller for the data it collects for operating the Marketplace, payments, fraud prevention, support and other purposes in our Privacy Notice. Where AfroTransact processes personal data on your behalf (for example hosting your catalogue or staff data), the Data Processing Terms apply.\n\n19.2 Your obligations. You must use Customer data only to fulfil the Order, provide support for it and comply with law; not sell or share it, or use it for marketing or profiling; keep it secure; not retain it longer than needed; delete it as the Data Processing Terms require; give Customers their privacy rights directly where you are their controller; and tell us promptly if you suffer a security incident affecting Customer data.\n\n19.3 Customer messages are sent via the Marketplace. You may not add Customers to your own marketing lists without their separate, lawful opt-in outside of order data.\n\n19.4 Data about you. We process the data you give us as described in the Privacy Notice and the Sub-processors list, including to verify identity, assess risk, pay you and comply with law. We may share your business details and information about disputes with Stripe, card networks, banks, law enforcement and regulators.\n\n19.5 Messaging consent. You must not use Marketplace contact details to send texts or WhatsApp messages unless the Customer has consented to receive them from you under applicable law.\n\n## 20. Warranties and your responsibility for your products\n\n20.1 You warrant that: the information you give us is true; you have the right to sell what you list; your products are safe, lawful, fit for the stated purpose, and as described; you comply with all applicable laws and these Terms; you are not subject to sanctions and are not owned or controlled by sanctioned persons; and you hold and keep valid all licences and insurance required.\n\n20.2 You are responsible for product liability and for the quality, safety, legality, description and delivery of your products. You must carry the insurance that is appropriate for your business [[LEGAL: specify any minimum product liability insurance required, by category]].\n\n20.3 We make no warranty about the volume of sales, the availability of the Marketplace or the conduct of Customers. The Marketplace is provided as available.\n\n## 21. Suspension, restriction and termination\n\n21.1 By us. We may limit, hide listings, pause payouts, suspend or terminate your account, with or without notice, if: you breach these Terms or a policy; we reasonably suspect fraud, illegality, counterfeiting, prohibited items or abuse; your performance falls below standard; Stripe or another provider restricts or closes your account or requires us to act; you fail verification or fail to keep information current; your licences or insurance lapse; there are excessive claims, chargebacks or complaints; we must comply with law, a court or regulator; you create risk to Customers, to us or to others; or you are inactive. We will tell you the reason where we can and where the law permits, and in cases of performance-based action, will give notice and an opportunity to respond or appeal before termination, except where urgent action is needed [[LEGAL: confirm notice and appeal commitments; these are derived from the existing seeded terms]].\n\n21.2 By you. You may close your account at any time, after you have fulfilled open Orders, paid what you owe, and completed any open claims. Plan fees already paid are not refunded unless we say so or the law requires.\n\n21.3 Effect. On termination or suspension: your listings are removed; open Orders must be fulfilled or are cancelled by us; payouts of remaining amounts are made after the Hold Window and after we have deducted what you owe and any reserve we need for expected claims and chargebacks (we may hold a reserve for up to [[LEGAL: reserve retention period after termination, for example 90 to 180 days]]); and sections that by nature continue will survive, including 6, 11 to 16, 18 to 20 and 22 to 27.\n\n21.4 Suspension and termination do not relieve you of responsibility for Orders you accepted or amounts you owe.\n\n## 22. Indemnity\n\n22.1 You will defend, indemnify and hold harmless AfroTransact, its affiliates, officers, employees and agents from claims, losses, liabilities, penalties, damages, costs and expenses (including reasonable legal fees) arising out of or related to: your products, listings and content; your breach of these Terms, the Payment Terms or the law; your taxes; Customer or third-party claims relating to your products, delivery or conduct; product liability, recalls and food safety; infringement claims about your content; your use of Customer data; and your negligence or wilful misconduct.\n\n22.2 We will tell you promptly of a claim and cooperate at your cost. You may not settle a claim that imposes an obligation on us or admits fault without our consent.\n\n## 23. Disclaimers and limitation of liability\n\n23.1 [[LEGAL: disclaimers drafted to the governing law; confirm \"as is\" language and conspicuous formatting required by law]] The Marketplace and our services are provided \"as is\" and \"as available\". To the fullest extent permitted by law we disclaim all implied warranties, including merchantability, fitness for a particular purpose and non-infringement.\n\n23.2 To the fullest extent permitted by law, AfroTransact and its affiliates will not be liable to you for indirect, incidental, special, consequential, exemplary or punitive damages, or for lost profits, revenue, goodwill or data, even if advised of the possibility.\n\n23.3 AfroTransact''s total liability for all claims relating to these Terms and the Marketplace will not exceed [[LEGAL: liability cap, for example the fees you paid us in the 12 months before the claim or a fixed amount]].\n\n23.4 Nothing excludes liability that cannot be excluded by law, including for fraud or wilful misconduct. [[LEGAL: carve-outs and state-specific rules]]\n\n23.5 We are not responsible for failures of Stripe, carriers, communications providers, hosting providers or other third parties beyond our reasonable control, or for events outside our control.\n\n## 24. Disputes with AfroTransact, governing law and venue\n\n24.1 [[LEGAL: governing law]] governs these Terms.\n\n24.2 [[LEGAL: exclusive venue / courts; or arbitration provision, class-action waiver, small-claims carve-out, opt-out period and arbitration provider]]\n\n24.3 Before starting a formal proceeding you will send us written notice of the dispute at {legal} and give us thirty (30) days to resolve it [[LEGAL: confirm informal resolution period]].\n\n## 25. Changes to these Terms\n\n25.1 We may change these Terms, and the policies and documents they incorporate, by posting the new version in Seller Center or notifying you. For material changes we give at least thirty (30) days'' notice [[LEGAL: confirm notice period]], except where a shorter period is required by law, by Stripe or by a regulator, or where the change corrects an error or benefits you.\n\n25.2 If you keep selling after the effective date, you accept the new version. If you do not accept, you may close your account before the effective date.\n\n25.3 Each version is archived with its effective date and a summary of changes. A certified copy of earlier versions is available on request at {legal}.\n\n## 26. Records and cooperation\n\n26.1 Keep your Order, tax, licence, insurance, sourcing and delivery records for at least the longer of seven (7) years and the period required by law [[LEGAL: confirm retention period; the Privacy Notice states 7 years for order records]]. Provide records to us on request within a reasonable time for audits, tax, compliance, disputes and regulator requests.\n\n26.2 You will cooperate with our investigations and with any regulator or law-enforcement request that concerns your activity.\n\n## 27. General\n\n27.1 Independent contractors. You are an independent business. Nothing creates a partnership, agency, franchise, employment or joint venture. You decide how to run your business, subject to these Terms.\n\n27.2 Assignment. You may not assign these Terms without our consent. We may assign them to an affiliate or a successor.\n\n27.3 Notices. We may notify you in Seller Center, by email or by text message to your contact details. You must keep them current. Send notices to us at {legal} and at {address}.\n\n27.4 Entire agreement and severability. These Terms and the documents they incorporate are the whole agreement on their subject. If a provision is unenforceable the rest continues.\n\n27.5 No waiver. Not enforcing a right is not a waiver.\n\n27.6 Force majeure. Neither party is liable for delay or failure caused by events beyond its reasonable control, except for payment obligations.\n\n27.7 Language. The English version governs.\n\n27.8 Export, sanctions and anti-corruption. You will comply with applicable sanctions, export-control and anti-corruption laws.\n\n27.9 Third-party beneficiaries. Stripe may enforce the parts of these Terms that relate to its services as stated in the Payment Terms. Otherwise there are no third-party beneficiaries.\n\n27.10 Contact. Questions go to {legal}. Our address is {address}."}'::jsonb, change_summary = 'Full rewrite of the seeded Seller & Provider Terms 2.4. Splits service-provider terms, payment terms, fees summary, data-processing terms and the sub-processor list into their own documents; adds two-step onboarding review, billing waterfall, delivery proof, the support and evidence dispute process, reserves and offsets, tax roles and data-protection roles.', created_at = now(), updated_at = now()
    where doc_type = 'seller-terms' and (major, minor, patch) = (1, 0, 0) and status = 'CURRENT'
      and created_by in ('seed', 'legal-loader') and updated_at = created_at;

-- prohibited-items 1.0 (effective 2026-12-01; locales: en; draft banner on)
delete from legal_version where doc_type = 'prohibited-items' and created_by = 'seed'
    and exists (select 1 from legal_version c where c.doc_type = 'prohibited-items' and c.status = 'CURRENT'
                and c.created_by = 'seed' and c.updated_at = c.created_at and (c.major, c.minor, c.patch) > (1, 0, 0));
