select '7f8bc410-899f-5f07-b68b-462e5a58f851'::uuid, 'provider-terms', '1.0', 1, 0, 0, '2026-12-01'::date, 'CURRENT', true,
       '{"en": "# Service Provider Terms\n\n> DRAFT. This document was prepared with AI assistance and has not been reviewed by qualified counsel. It must be reviewed and approved by counsel before it is published or relied on. Text in double square brackets marked LEGAL is an open decision.\n\n## 1. Agreement and how these terms fit together\n\n1.1 These Service Provider Terms are a contract between you (the business or individual that applies for or holds a provider account, \"Provider\", \"you\") and [[LEGAL: contracting entity name, form, state of organisation and registered address for AfroTransact]] (\"AfroTransact\", \"we\", \"us\").\n\n1.2 They incorporate, and you also accept, the Seller Terms (for the sections on accounts, onboarding review, reserves and offsets, taxes, prohibited conduct, intellectual property, data protection, suspension, indemnity, liability, governing law and changes), the Payment Terms, the Data Processing Terms, the Seller Fees Summary and the Sub-processors list. In this document, \"Seller Terms\" sections apply to Providers as if \"Seller\" meant \"Provider\", \"Order\" included \"Booking\", and \"Hold Window\" meant the hold after service completion. If these terms conflict with the Seller Terms about services, these terms control. The Payment Terms control on money movement.\n\n1.3 Definitions: \"Service\" is anything you offer for booking, whether in person, at the customer''s home or virtual. \"Booking\" is a customer''s reservation of a Service at a time. \"Customer\" is the person booking. \"Practitioner\" is an individual who performs a Service for you (an owner, employee or contractor). \"Provider Hub\" is your back office. \"Deposit\" is the part of the price charged to confirm a Booking. \"Balance\" is the remainder.\n\n## 2. Eligibility, onboarding and regulated services\n\n2.1 The two-step onboarding review in the Seller Terms applies. For services we also collect licence and insurance information for regulated services (such as licence number, issuing state, expiry date, licence document and proof of insurance), and we may decline categories we do not support.\n\n2.2 Services that require a licence, certification, registration or permit (for example beauty and personal-care licences, health or wellness practice, food preparation, childcare, transportation, immigration, tax or legal services where permitted) may be offered only if you and each Practitioner hold, and keep in force, all required authorisations. You must tell us immediately of any lapse, suspension, restriction or complaint to a licensing body.\n\n2.3 Some services are not permitted or need our approval (see section 13 and the Prohibited & Restricted Items policy). Services that Stripe prohibits or restricts for payment processing (see the Payment Terms) may not be offered, and offering them is a breach.\n\n2.4 You must operate in a live market and service area as we notify you.\n\n## 3. Warranties: licensing, insurance and competence\n\n3.1 You warrant that: you and each Practitioner are qualified, licensed, registered and (where required) background-checked for the Services; you have at all times the insurance appropriate to the Services, including professional or general liability insurance [[LEGAL: minimum coverage amounts by service category and whether AfroTransact must be named as additional insured]]; you comply with health, safety, sanitation, accessibility, privacy and consumer laws; and each Service is performed with reasonable skill and care, as described.\n\n3.2 You must give us proof of licences and insurance on request and when they renew. We may hide Services whose licences or insurance have expired.\n\n3.3 You are solely responsible for the quality, safety and legality of your Services and for any injury, loss or damage caused in providing them. AfroTransact does not supervise or direct how you perform Services and is not the provider of your Services.\n\n3.4 You are responsible for the safety of Customers on your premises and for your conduct, and your Practitioners'' conduct, at Customer premises. Services at a Customer''s home must be performed with identification, and you must follow the safety practices we publish.\n\n## 4. Services and listings\n\n4.1 You describe each Service accurately: what is included, duration, price, deposit, mode (in person, at home or virtual), add-ons, preparation required, who performs it, location, cancellation policy and any restrictions (age, health conditions, allergies). Bookings copy the Service details at the time of booking, so later changes do not affect existing Bookings.\n\n4.2 Listings are moderated as in the Seller Terms. We may hide or remove Services at any time for policy, safety or risk reasons.\n\n4.3 You must not misrepresent qualifications or results, use before-and-after claims you cannot substantiate, or make medical or disease-treatment claims unless lawful and approved.\n\n4.4 Content that Customers or you upload (photos, notes) is subject to the Intellectual Property sections of the Seller Terms.\n\n## 5. Practitioners and team members\n\n5.1 You may add team members to Provider Hub with roles (owner, manager, practitioner, finance). Each Practitioner has their own working hours and time off. Practitioners see only their own calendar unless given broader permission.\n\n5.2 You are responsible for your team members as for yourself: their licences, insurance, background checks where required, training, wages and benefits, taxes and conduct. They are your employees or contractors, never ours. Misclassification and employment compliance are your responsibility.\n\n5.3 You must ensure that team members who use Provider Hub follow our security rules, only access data they need, and are removed when they leave.\n\n5.4 We may ask you to remove or replace a Practitioner whose conduct or authorisations create risk.\n\n## 6. Availability, bookings and confirmation\n\n6.1 You keep your availability current: working hours, time off, notice period, booking horizon, buffers and daily limits. Slots shown to Customers are generated from them. We try to prevent double booking but you are responsible for your calendar.\n\n6.2 Instant bookings are confirmed automatically. For booking requests, you must accept or decline within the response time shown (at the time of drafting, four hours, but it is configured by Staff). If you do not respond the request expires and any hold on the Customer''s payment is released.\n\n6.3 Once you confirm a Booking you must perform the Service at the agreed time and place. Reschedule requests must be handled promptly; a proposed new time requires the Customer''s acceptance.\n\n6.4 Do not take bookings you cannot honour. Bookings you cancel count against your standing.\n\n## 7. Prices, deposits and balance\n\n7.1 The Customer''s payment is authorised when they book. The Deposit is captured when the Booking is confirmed. The Balance is captured on completion or, for bookings far in the future, charged on the Customer''s saved card shortly before the appointment (at the time of drafting, two days before) by Stripe on our behalf, with the Customer''s consent obtained at booking. If the balance cannot be charged, the Booking may be flagged as unpaid and you may be allowed to cancel it.\n\n7.2 You may not request payment from Customers outside the Marketplace for a Service booked on it, and you may not charge Customers extra fees not disclosed at booking.\n\n7.3 A Service with no Deposit is charged in full on the same schedule as a Balance.\n\n7.4 Prices are in US dollars and include everything except tax, which we calculate, collect and remit where required under the Seller Terms. [[LEGAL: confirm sales tax treatment of services by state and whether AfroTransact collects any tax on service bookings]]\n\n## 8. Cancellation, rescheduling and no-show\n\n8.1 Policies. You choose a cancellation policy for your Services from those we offer, such as Flexible, Moderate, Strict or Custom (a free-cancellation window, an optional partial refund, a no-show rule and a reschedule window and limit). The policy that applied when the Booking was made stays with that Booking. The Customer sees the exact refund before they cancel.\n\n8.2 The standard policies work as follows at the time of drafting, and Staff may update them: Flexible, free cancellation until 24 hours before; Moderate, free until 48 hours before and half the Deposit kept between 24 and 48 hours; Strict, free until 7 days before. Custom policies must be within the limits we allow.\n\n8.3 Customer cancellation. If the Customer cancels outside the free window, the part of the Deposit that your policy says you keep is paid to you (less fees) after the hold. Everything else is refunded.\n\n8.4 No-show. If the Customer does not attend, you may mark a no-show after the start time. Your policy decides whether you keep the Deposit, or the full price, or nothing. You must mark no-shows honestly and in good time; false no-shows are fraud. A Customer may dispute a no-show through support, and Staff decide using the evidence.\n\n8.5 Provider cancellation. If you cancel or do not attend, the Customer receives a full refund including any Deposit. A late cancellation by you, or a failure to attend, may incur a provider late-cancellation fee and affects your standing. The fee amount and timing are configured by Staff and shown in Provider Hub. We may recover the fee from your balance or your connected account.\n\n8.6 Customer cancellation by us. We may cancel a Booking for fraud, safety, legal or operational reasons. Refunds follow what is fair; we may also refund Customers if your conduct caused the problem and recover the cost from you.\n\n8.7 Completion. A Booking is completed when you mark it complete or, if you do not, automatically after a period after the appointment (at the time of drafting, 48 hours) unless the Customer has raised a problem.\n\n8.8 Refunds for poor service. A Customer who is unhappy with a completed Service may raise a help request with evidence. Staff decide the outcome as set out in the Seller Terms (support and evidence disputes) and may refund all or part of the price. Any refund attributable to you is recovered from you as in the Seller Terms.\n\n## 9. Fees: plan fee and connection fee\n\n9.1 You pay a plan fee (if your plan has one) and a connection fee. The connection fee is a percentage of the service money that AfroTransact actually captures for you, charged when it is captured. The percentage is configured by Staff and can depend on your plan, the service category and rates negotiated with you in writing. The Seller Fees Summary explains how this works. Rates are not fixed in these terms.\n\n9.2 Timing: the connection fee applies to the Deposit when captured, to the Balance when captured, and to a Deposit or no-show amount you keep after a late cancellation or no-show. Nothing is charged when a Booking is cancelled or declined before anything is captured. On a refund, the connection fee is returned in proportion where the fee schedule says so; otherwise AfroTransact keeps it.\n\n9.3 Provider Hub shows an estimate of what you keep before you accept a request: the price less the connection fee and estimated card fees. Estimates are not guarantees.\n\n9.4 Plan fees and the billing waterfall work as set out in the Seller Terms.\n\n9.5 AfroTransact Retail does not pay a connection fee. This does not affect yours.\n\n## 10. Payouts\n\n10.1 Payouts are made through Stripe as set out in the Payment Terms and the Seller Terms. For a Service, the hold window starts when the Booking is completed (or a kept Deposit or no-show amount is settled), and lasts for the default period or the period Staff configure. After the hold, the net amount (price captured less connection fee and allocated card fees) is transferred to your connected account.\n\n10.2 Payouts can be held, reserved, reduced or offset as in the Seller Terms (holds, reserves, offsets, chargebacks and negative balances), including for open complaints, disputes, chargebacks, late-cancellation fees and licensing lapses.\n\n10.3 You are responsible for your own income tax, self-employment tax and registration. We report payments as required.\n\n## 11. Reviews\n\n11.1 Customers can rate and review completed Bookings. Reviews are checked and may be moderated by Staff before they are public. Approved reviews feed ratings and search.\n\n11.2 You may reply once to a published review. You may not edit, hide, delete, trade, buy, incentivise or write reviews, ask Customers to withdraw a review, or retaliate. You may report a review for moderation.\n\n11.3 Replies must be civil, factual and must not disclose health information or other personal data about the Customer.\n\n## 12. Customer data and health or sensitive information\n\n12.1 Data protection roles are in the Seller Terms and the Data Processing Terms. For Customer data you receive to perform a Booking (name, contact details, address, appointment notes and information about the Service), you are an independent controller of that data for performing the Service and meeting your legal obligations.\n\n12.2 Services may involve sensitive information (for example health, appearance or religion). You must collect only what you need, tell the Customer how you use it, obtain any consent that law requires, keep it secure, and keep it separate from Marketplace messaging. You must comply with any health privacy or professional-confidentiality laws that apply to you, which are your responsibility. AfroTransact does not provide a service for protected health information [[LEGAL: confirm HIPAA and state health privacy positioning, and whether AfroTransact should prohibit or limit health-related services]].\n\n12.3 Use Marketplace messaging for Booking communications. Do not market to Customers or move them off-platform.\n\n12.4 Recording, photography and virtual sessions must follow the law and Customer consent. Virtual Services must use a platform that meets your professional and legal obligations.\n\n## 13. Prohibited and restricted services\n\n13.1 You must not offer: anything illegal; sexual services or adult entertainment; services requiring a licence you do not hold; weapons training or sales; gambling; services that facilitate fraud or harm; unlicensed financial, money-transmission or legal advice or immigration services; unlicensed medical, pharmaceutical or telehealth services; and any service listed in the Prohibited & Restricted Items policy or on Stripe''s list of prohibited and restricted businesses [[LEGAL: confirm which regulated services (for example immigration, legal, tax, health, travel, financial) AfroTransact will allow, and under what conditions]].\n\n13.2 We may remove services and accounts immediately for breach.\n\n## 14. Safety and conduct\n\n14.1 You and your team must treat Customers with respect, follow our Community Guidelines, and not discriminate on the basis of any protected characteristic in violation of law.\n\n14.2 Report safety incidents involving a Customer or Practitioner to us promptly. We may suspend while we investigate.\n\n14.3 You are responsible for background checks and safety at at-home and in-person Services, and for compliance with local laws on identity verification of workers.\n\n## 15. Insurance, indemnity and liability\n\n15.1 Your indemnity, our disclaimer and the limitation of liability in the Seller Terms apply to the Services. Without limiting that, you will defend and indemnify AfroTransact against claims arising from your Services, your Practitioners, injury, damage, misrepresentation and breach of licensing law.\n\n15.2 [[LEGAL: confirm whether any additional liability cap, safety-incident clause or special insurance requirement should apply to at-home services]]\n\n## 16. Suspension, termination and changes\n\n16.1 Suspension and termination follow the Seller Terms. In addition, we may suspend Services or accounts on a licensing lapse, a safety incident, repeated no-shows or late cancellations, or repeated Customer complaints.\n\n16.2 On termination, future Bookings are cancelled and Customers refunded unless we arrange for another provider; deposits already captured are handled according to the cancellation policy and the Seller Terms.\n\n16.3 We may change these Service Provider Terms as set out in the Seller Terms (notice and effective date).\n\n## 17. Governing law and contact\n\n17.1 Governing law, venue and dispute resolution are as set out in the Seller Terms: [[LEGAL: governing law, venue and any arbitration terms, consistent with the Seller Terms]].\n\n17.2 Questions: {legal}. Address: {address}."}'::jsonb, 'New document. Separates the terms for service providers and bookings (services, bookings, deposits, cancellations, no-shows, practitioners, licensing and insurance, reviews, connection fees, payouts) from the Seller Terms.', now(), 'legal-loader', now(), null
 where not exists (select 1 from legal_version where doc_type = 'provider-terms' and (major, minor, patch) >= (1, 0, 0));
update legal_version set effective_date = '2026-12-01'::date, draft_banner = true,
       bodies = '{"en": "# Service Provider Terms\n\n> DRAFT. This document was prepared with AI assistance and has not been reviewed by qualified counsel. It must be reviewed and approved by counsel before it is published or relied on. Text in double square brackets marked LEGAL is an open decision.\n\n## 1. Agreement and how these terms fit together\n\n1.1 These Service Provider Terms are a contract between you (the business or individual that applies for or holds a provider account, \"Provider\", \"you\") and [[LEGAL: contracting entity name, form, state of organisation and registered address for AfroTransact]] (\"AfroTransact\", \"we\", \"us\").\n\n1.2 They incorporate, and you also accept, the Seller Terms (for the sections on accounts, onboarding review, reserves and offsets, taxes, prohibited conduct, intellectual property, data protection, suspension, indemnity, liability, governing law and changes), the Payment Terms, the Data Processing Terms, the Seller Fees Summary and the Sub-processors list. In this document, \"Seller Terms\" sections apply to Providers as if \"Seller\" meant \"Provider\", \"Order\" included \"Booking\", and \"Hold Window\" meant the hold after service completion. If these terms conflict with the Seller Terms about services, these terms control. The Payment Terms control on money movement.\n\n1.3 Definitions: \"Service\" is anything you offer for booking, whether in person, at the customer''s home or virtual. \"Booking\" is a customer''s reservation of a Service at a time. \"Customer\" is the person booking. \"Practitioner\" is an individual who performs a Service for you (an owner, employee or contractor). \"Provider Hub\" is your back office. \"Deposit\" is the part of the price charged to confirm a Booking. \"Balance\" is the remainder.\n\n## 2. Eligibility, onboarding and regulated services\n\n2.1 The two-step onboarding review in the Seller Terms applies. For services we also collect licence and insurance information for regulated services (such as licence number, issuing state, expiry date, licence document and proof of insurance), and we may decline categories we do not support.\n\n2.2 Services that require a licence, certification, registration or permit (for example beauty and personal-care licences, health or wellness practice, food preparation, childcare, transportation, immigration, tax or legal services where permitted) may be offered only if you and each Practitioner hold, and keep in force, all required authorisations. You must tell us immediately of any lapse, suspension, restriction or complaint to a licensing body.\n\n2.3 Some services are not permitted or need our approval (see section 13 and the Prohibited & Restricted Items policy). Services that Stripe prohibits or restricts for payment processing (see the Payment Terms) may not be offered, and offering them is a breach.\n\n2.4 You must operate in a live market and service area as we notify you.\n\n## 3. Warranties: licensing, insurance and competence\n\n3.1 You warrant that: you and each Practitioner are qualified, licensed, registered and (where required) background-checked for the Services; you have at all times the insurance appropriate to the Services, including professional or general liability insurance [[LEGAL: minimum coverage amounts by service category and whether AfroTransact must be named as additional insured]]; you comply with health, safety, sanitation, accessibility, privacy and consumer laws; and each Service is performed with reasonable skill and care, as described.\n\n3.2 You must give us proof of licences and insurance on request and when they renew. We may hide Services whose licences or insurance have expired.\n\n3.3 You are solely responsible for the quality, safety and legality of your Services and for any injury, loss or damage caused in providing them. AfroTransact does not supervise or direct how you perform Services and is not the provider of your Services.\n\n3.4 You are responsible for the safety of Customers on your premises and for your conduct, and your Practitioners'' conduct, at Customer premises. Services at a Customer''s home must be performed with identification, and you must follow the safety practices we publish.\n\n## 4. Services and listings\n\n4.1 You describe each Service accurately: what is included, duration, price, deposit, mode (in person, at home or virtual), add-ons, preparation required, who performs it, location, cancellation policy and any restrictions (age, health conditions, allergies). Bookings copy the Service details at the time of booking, so later changes do not affect existing Bookings.\n\n4.2 Listings are moderated as in the Seller Terms. We may hide or remove Services at any time for policy, safety or risk reasons.\n\n4.3 You must not misrepresent qualifications or results, use before-and-after claims you cannot substantiate, or make medical or disease-treatment claims unless lawful and approved.\n\n4.4 Content that Customers or you upload (photos, notes) is subject to the Intellectual Property sections of the Seller Terms.\n\n## 5. Practitioners and team members\n\n5.1 You may add team members to Provider Hub with roles (owner, manager, practitioner, finance). Each Practitioner has their own working hours and time off. Practitioners see only their own calendar unless given broader permission.\n\n5.2 You are responsible for your team members as for yourself: their licences, insurance, background checks where required, training, wages and benefits, taxes and conduct. They are your employees or contractors, never ours. Misclassification and employment compliance are your responsibility.\n\n5.3 You must ensure that team members who use Provider Hub follow our security rules, only access data they need, and are removed when they leave.\n\n5.4 We may ask you to remove or replace a Practitioner whose conduct or authorisations create risk.\n\n## 6. Availability, bookings and confirmation\n\n6.1 You keep your availability current: working hours, time off, notice period, booking horizon, buffers and daily limits. Slots shown to Customers are generated from them. We try to prevent double booking but you are responsible for your calendar.\n\n6.2 Instant bookings are confirmed automatically. For booking requests, you must accept or decline within the response time shown (at the time of drafting, four hours, but it is configured by Staff). If you do not respond the request expires and any hold on the Customer''s payment is released.\n\n6.3 Once you confirm a Booking you must perform the Service at the agreed time and place. Reschedule requests must be handled promptly; a proposed new time requires the Customer''s acceptance.\n\n6.4 Do not take bookings you cannot honour. Bookings you cancel count against your standing.\n\n## 7. Prices, deposits and balance\n\n7.1 The Customer''s payment is authorised when they book. The Deposit is captured when the Booking is confirmed. The Balance is captured on completion or, for bookings far in the future, charged on the Customer''s saved card shortly before the appointment (at the time of drafting, two days before) by Stripe on our behalf, with the Customer''s consent obtained at booking. If the balance cannot be charged, the Booking may be flagged as unpaid and you may be allowed to cancel it.\n\n7.2 You may not request payment from Customers outside the Marketplace for a Service booked on it, and you may not charge Customers extra fees not disclosed at booking.\n\n7.3 A Service with no Deposit is charged in full on the same schedule as a Balance.\n\n7.4 Prices are in US dollars and include everything except tax, which we calculate, collect and remit where required under the Seller Terms. [[LEGAL: confirm sales tax treatment of services by state and whether AfroTransact collects any tax on service bookings]]\n\n## 8. Cancellation, rescheduling and no-show\n\n8.1 Policies. You choose a cancellation policy for your Services from those we offer, such as Flexible, Moderate, Strict or Custom (a free-cancellation window, an optional partial refund, a no-show rule and a reschedule window and limit). The policy that applied when the Booking was made stays with that Booking. The Customer sees the exact refund before they cancel.\n\n8.2 The standard policies work as follows at the time of drafting, and Staff may update them: Flexible, free cancellation until 24 hours before; Moderate, free until 48 hours before and half the Deposit kept between 24 and 48 hours; Strict, free until 7 days before. Custom policies must be within the limits we allow.\n\n8.3 Customer cancellation. If the Customer cancels outside the free window, the part of the Deposit that your policy says you keep is paid to you (less fees) after the hold. Everything else is refunded.\n\n8.4 No-show. If the Customer does not attend, you may mark a no-show after the start time. Your policy decides whether you keep the Deposit, or the full price, or nothing. You must mark no-shows honestly and in good time; false no-shows are fraud. A Customer may dispute a no-show through support, and Staff decide using the evidence.\n\n8.5 Provider cancellation. If you cancel or do not attend, the Customer receives a full refund including any Deposit. A late cancellation by you, or a failure to attend, may incur a provider late-cancellation fee and affects your standing. The fee amount and timing are configured by Staff and shown in Provider Hub. We may recover the fee from your balance or your connected account.\n\n8.6 Customer cancellation by us. We may cancel a Booking for fraud, safety, legal or operational reasons. Refunds follow what is fair; we may also refund Customers if your conduct caused the problem and recover the cost from you.\n\n8.7 Completion. A Booking is completed when you mark it complete or, if you do not, automatically after a period after the appointment (at the time of drafting, 48 hours) unless the Customer has raised a problem.\n\n8.8 Refunds for poor service. A Customer who is unhappy with a completed Service may raise a help request with evidence. Staff decide the outcome as set out in the Seller Terms (support and evidence disputes) and may refund all or part of the price. Any refund attributable to you is recovered from you as in the Seller Terms.\n\n## 9. Fees: plan fee and connection fee\n\n9.1 You pay a plan fee (if your plan has one) and a connection fee. The connection fee is a percentage of the service money that AfroTransact actually captures for you, charged when it is captured. The percentage is configured by Staff and can depend on your plan, the service category and rates negotiated with you in writing. The Seller Fees Summary explains how this works. Rates are not fixed in these terms.\n\n9.2 Timing: the connection fee applies to the Deposit when captured, to the Balance when captured, and to a Deposit or no-show amount you keep after a late cancellation or no-show. Nothing is charged when a Booking is cancelled or declined before anything is captured. On a refund, the connection fee is returned in proportion where the fee schedule says so; otherwise AfroTransact keeps it.\n\n9.3 Provider Hub shows an estimate of what you keep before you accept a request: the price less the connection fee and estimated card fees. Estimates are not guarantees.\n\n9.4 Plan fees and the billing waterfall work as set out in the Seller Terms.\n\n9.5 AfroTransact Retail does not pay a connection fee. This does not affect yours.\n\n## 10. Payouts\n\n10.1 Payouts are made through Stripe as set out in the Payment Terms and the Seller Terms. For a Service, the hold window starts when the Booking is completed (or a kept Deposit or no-show amount is settled), and lasts for the default period or the period Staff configure. After the hold, the net amount (price captured less connection fee and allocated card fees) is transferred to your connected account.\n\n10.2 Payouts can be held, reserved, reduced or offset as in the Seller Terms (holds, reserves, offsets, chargebacks and negative balances), including for open complaints, disputes, chargebacks, late-cancellation fees and licensing lapses.\n\n10.3 You are responsible for your own income tax, self-employment tax and registration. We report payments as required.\n\n## 11. Reviews\n\n11.1 Customers can rate and review completed Bookings. Reviews are checked and may be moderated by Staff before they are public. Approved reviews feed ratings and search.\n\n11.2 You may reply once to a published review. You may not edit, hide, delete, trade, buy, incentivise or write reviews, ask Customers to withdraw a review, or retaliate. You may report a review for moderation.\n\n11.3 Replies must be civil, factual and must not disclose health information or other personal data about the Customer.\n\n## 12. Customer data and health or sensitive information\n\n12.1 Data protection roles are in the Seller Terms and the Data Processing Terms. For Customer data you receive to perform a Booking (name, contact details, address, appointment notes and information about the Service), you are an independent controller of that data for performing the Service and meeting your legal obligations.\n\n12.2 Services may involve sensitive information (for example health, appearance or religion). You must collect only what you need, tell the Customer how you use it, obtain any consent that law requires, keep it secure, and keep it separate from Marketplace messaging. You must comply with any health privacy or professional-confidentiality laws that apply to you, which are your responsibility. AfroTransact does not provide a service for protected health information [[LEGAL: confirm HIPAA and state health privacy positioning, and whether AfroTransact should prohibit or limit health-related services]].\n\n12.3 Use Marketplace messaging for Booking communications. Do not market to Customers or move them off-platform.\n\n12.4 Recording, photography and virtual sessions must follow the law and Customer consent. Virtual Services must use a platform that meets your professional and legal obligations.\n\n## 13. Prohibited and restricted services\n\n13.1 You must not offer: anything illegal; sexual services or adult entertainment; services requiring a licence you do not hold; weapons training or sales; gambling; services that facilitate fraud or harm; unlicensed financial, money-transmission or legal advice or immigration services; unlicensed medical, pharmaceutical or telehealth services; and any service listed in the Prohibited & Restricted Items policy or on Stripe''s list of prohibited and restricted businesses [[LEGAL: confirm which regulated services (for example immigration, legal, tax, health, travel, financial) AfroTransact will allow, and under what conditions]].\n\n13.2 We may remove services and accounts immediately for breach.\n\n## 14. Safety and conduct\n\n14.1 You and your team must treat Customers with respect, follow our Community Guidelines, and not discriminate on the basis of any protected characteristic in violation of law.\n\n14.2 Report safety incidents involving a Customer or Practitioner to us promptly. We may suspend while we investigate.\n\n14.3 You are responsible for background checks and safety at at-home and in-person Services, and for compliance with local laws on identity verification of workers.\n\n## 15. Insurance, indemnity and liability\n\n15.1 Your indemnity, our disclaimer and the limitation of liability in the Seller Terms apply to the Services. Without limiting that, you will defend and indemnify AfroTransact against claims arising from your Services, your Practitioners, injury, damage, misrepresentation and breach of licensing law.\n\n15.2 [[LEGAL: confirm whether any additional liability cap, safety-incident clause or special insurance requirement should apply to at-home services]]\n\n## 16. Suspension, termination and changes\n\n16.1 Suspension and termination follow the Seller Terms. In addition, we may suspend Services or accounts on a licensing lapse, a safety incident, repeated no-shows or late cancellations, or repeated Customer complaints.\n\n16.2 On termination, future Bookings are cancelled and Customers refunded unless we arrange for another provider; deposits already captured are handled according to the cancellation policy and the Seller Terms.\n\n16.3 We may change these Service Provider Terms as set out in the Seller Terms (notice and effective date).\n\n## 17. Governing law and contact\n\n17.1 Governing law, venue and dispute resolution are as set out in the Seller Terms: [[LEGAL: governing law, venue and any arbitration terms, consistent with the Seller Terms]].\n\n17.2 Questions: {legal}. Address: {address}."}'::jsonb, change_summary = 'New document. Separates the terms for service providers and bookings (services, bookings, deposits, cancellations, no-shows, practitioners, licensing and insurance, reviews, connection fees, payouts) from the Seller Terms.', created_at = now(), updated_at = now()
    where doc_type = 'provider-terms' and (major, minor, patch) = (1, 0, 0) and status = 'CURRENT'
      and created_by in ('seed', 'legal-loader') and updated_at = created_at;

-- payments-terms 1.0 (effective 2026-12-01; locales: en; draft banner on)
delete from legal_version where doc_type = 'payments-terms' and created_by = 'seed'
    and exists (select 1 from legal_version c where c.doc_type = 'payments-terms' and c.status = 'CURRENT'
                and c.created_by = 'seed' and c.updated_at = c.created_at and (c.major, c.minor, c.patch) > (1, 0, 0));
update legal_version set status = 'ARCHIVED', archived_at = now(), updated_at = now()
    where doc_type = 'payments-terms' and status = 'CURRENT' and (major, minor, patch) < (1, 0, 0);
insert into legal_version (id, doc_type, version, major, minor, patch, effective_date, status, draft_banner, bodies,
                           change_summary, created_at, created_by, updated_at, archived_at)
select '4012f7c7-0bfc-5257-a925-c3160587f5ee'::uuid, 'payments-terms', '1.0', 1, 0, 0, '2026-12-01'::date, 'CURRENT', true,
       '{"en": "# Payment Terms for Sellers and Providers\n\n> DRAFT. This document was prepared with AI assistance and has not been reviewed by qualified counsel. It must be reviewed and approved by counsel before it is published or relied on. Text in double square brackets marked LEGAL is an open decision. Descriptions of third-party terms are summaries, not substitutes for the third party''s own text.\n\n## 1. Scope and order of priority\n\n1.1 These Payment Terms apply to everything about how money moves between customers, AfroTransact and you: payment acceptance, settlement, payouts, holds, reserves, refunds, chargebacks, fees and negative balances. They are part of the Seller Terms and the Service Provider Terms (together, the \"Marketplace Terms\"). For anything about money movement these Payment Terms control over the Marketplace Terms.\n\n1.2 \"We\" and \"AfroTransact\" mean [[LEGAL: contracting entity name]]. \"Stripe\" means the Stripe entity that provides payment services to you under the Stripe Agreements in section 3. \"Connected Account\" means your Stripe account created through AfroTransact.\n\n## 2. Who does what\n\n2.1 Payment processing is provided by Stripe. AfroTransact is a platform that uses Stripe Connect to accept customer payments for your sales and to send you your share. AfroTransact is not a bank. Your connected account is held with Stripe, not with AfroTransact.\n\n2.2 Customers pay AfroTransact''s checkout. We authorise the payment when an order or booking is placed and capture it as described in the Marketplace Terms (for products, when a package leaves for delivery; for services, a deposit when the booking is confirmed and the balance on completion). We then settle your share to your Connected Account after the hold window in section 6.\n\n2.3 [[LEGAL: money-transmission positioning. Confirm, with licensing counsel, whether AfroTransact''s receipt of customer funds and onward transfer to sellers and providers is structured as an agent-of-payee arrangement, a payment facilitator or marketplace exemption, or otherwise, and what state licences or registrations are or are not required. Do not publish this section until confirmed.]] We state that, as between you and us, we receive payments as the platform that facilitates your sale and settle your share through Stripe Connect.\n\n2.4 Where AfroTransact Retail sells as seller of record, the sale proceeds belong to AfroTransact Retail; there is no payout or transfer to it as if it were a third-party seller.\n\n2.5 We are not responsible for Stripe''s acts or omissions, but we will cooperate to resolve problems. Nothing in these Payment Terms makes Stripe a party to the Marketplace Terms except where those Terms say Stripe may rely on them.\n\n## 3. Stripe agreements you accept\n\n3.1 To receive payments you must have a Connected Account and accept Stripe''s terms. Your Connected Account is opened through embedded onboarding inside Seller Center or Provider Hub. By opening it you agree to the Stripe Connected Account Agreement, which incorporates the Stripe Services Agreement, and Stripe''s privacy policy [[LEGAL: confirm with counsel and Stripe''s current documents which Stripe service agreement applies to the connected account configuration used: Stripe verifies identity, Stripe is liable for negative balances of the connected account, the account pays Stripe''s fees, and there is no Stripe dashboard. Record the agreement version, date and jurisdiction version accepted by each account]]. Links will be shown at the point of acceptance:\n- Stripe Connected Account Agreement: https://stripe.com/legal/connect-account\n- Stripe Services Agreement: https://stripe.com/legal/ssa\n- Stripe Privacy Center: https://stripe.com/privacy\n\n3.2 The Stripe agreements are contracts between you and Stripe. We cannot change them or waive them. If they conflict with the Marketplace Terms about Stripe''s own rights and services, the Stripe agreements control as between you and Stripe. We may suspend your payouts or listings if Stripe closes, restricts or disables your Connected Account.\n\n3.3 You must comply with Stripe''s rules at all times, including its list of prohibited and restricted businesses (https://stripe.com/legal/restricted-businesses) and the card network rules that Stripe passes on. A business or product that Stripe prohibits cannot be sold or provided through AfroTransact, even if our own policies would otherwise allow it. Stripe may change its lists; the current list applies.\n\n3.4 Stripe may change its agreements and services. We are not liable for those changes, but we will tell you of changes to the Stripe requirements that we learn about and that affect you.\n\n## 4. Verification (KYC / KYB) and information\n\n4.1 You must complete Stripe''s identity and business verification before payouts are enabled. Stripe collects and verifies the information it needs (for example the legal name, tax identification number, date of birth, address, beneficial owners, and your payout bank account). You may be asked to supply more at any time, including when your volume grows.\n\n4.2 You must provide accurate, complete and up-to-date information to Stripe and to us, and respond by Stripe''s deadlines. If verification items become past due, Stripe may disable your charges or payouts, and we will pause payouts and hide your listings until they are resolved.\n\n4.3 Stripe collects the verification data and the payout bank account itself, through its own components in Seller Center or Provider Hub. AfroTransact does not store your full bank account number or full identity documents. We receive verification results, status flags and the last digits of your account for display and compliance. Tax identification numbers you give us during onboarding are stored encrypted and visible in masked form to reviewers.\n\n4.4 We may also run our own checks (including sanctions and adverse-media screening, document review and the two-step review in the Seller Terms). Stripe''s verification does not replace ours.\n\n4.5 You must tell us and Stripe promptly if your ownership, control, business model or category changes.\n\n## 5. Fees and who bears them\n\n5.1 AfroTransact fees (plan fee, commission, connection fee, labels, handling, delivery) are described in the Seller Terms, the Service Provider Terms and the Seller Fees Summary and are configured by Staff.\n\n5.2 Stripe''s processing fees on each payment and Stripe''s account and payout fees are charged under the Stripe agreements. As configured, your Connected Account bears Stripe''s account and payout fees, and the processing cost of each payment is allocated to the lines it relates to and shown on your statement. The exact allocation is shown in Seller Center.\n\n5.3 We may deduct our fees and amounts under section 8 from payments, transfers and payouts, from your Connected Account where Stripe permits, or invoice you.\n\n5.4 Currency. Customers pay in US dollars. Payouts are made in the currency and to the bank accounts Stripe supports for your Connected Account. Stripe may charge conversion fees.\n\n## 6. Settlement, hold window and payouts\n\n6.1 Authorisation and capture. For products, the payment is captured per shipment when the package is collected or leaves for delivery. For services, see the Service Provider Terms.\n\n6.2 Hold window. After delivery (or service completion) your net earnings for that order are held by AfroTransact for a hold window. The default is seven (7) days and Staff may configure it. The hold covers time for delivery problems, complaints, claims, refunds and chargebacks. [[LEGAL: confirm that the hold is not characterised as an unlawful retention or as custody of funds requiring a licence; confirm notice requirements for changing the period]]\n\n6.3 Transfer. After the hold window, we transfer the net amount to your Connected Account through Stripe, after deducting commission, fees, refunds, chargebacks, label charges, plan fees collected from earnings (if you chose that option) and other amounts under section 8. Transfers may be made in several parts.\n\n6.4 Payout. Your Connected Account is set to manual payouts. You request a payout from Seller Center or Provider Hub, subject to the minimum amount and frequency shown there (at the time of drafting, USD 25 and one request per day). Requests may be reviewed and approved. Approved payouts are sent by Stripe to your verified bank account and arrive according to Stripe''s and your bank''s timing.\n\n6.5 Holds. We may delay, pause or hold payouts or transfers, in whole or in part: while verification items are due or past due; during a suspension; during a claim, help request, dispute or chargeback; if we suspect fraud, sanctions risk or a breach; on Stripe''s request; and as required by law, a court or a regulator. A hold stays for as long as the reason exists, and we will tell you the reason when we lawfully can.\n\n6.6 Reserves. We may require a reserve as set out in the Seller Terms. Stripe may also establish a reserve or delay payouts under the Stripe Services Agreement, in which case Stripe''s reserve terms (which Stripe notifies to you) apply and we cannot override them.\n\n6.7 No interest. No interest accrues on any funds we hold.\n\n6.8 Incorrect or failed payouts. If a payout fails (for example because your bank details are wrong) it is returned to your balance, and you must correct your details. We are not liable for delays caused by Stripe, your bank or incorrect details.\n\n6.9 Statements. Seller Center and Provider Hub show your balance, pending earnings, holds, transfers, payouts and deductions, per order, and statement exports. You must review them and tell us of errors within [[LEGAL: error-notification period, for example 60 days]].\n\n## 7. Refunds\n\n7.1 Refunds to customers follow the Marketplace Terms and the Returns & Refunds policy. When a refund is made, the original payment method is credited for the refunded amount (and a part may be returned as AfroTransact store credit where Staff decide so in a support resolution).\n\n7.2 The refund is charged to the party responsible: to you when attributable to you, otherwise to AfroTransact. A refund reduces your earnings by the refunded item amount, adjusted by the commission or connection fee returned in proportion where the schedule says so. If the refund happens after we have transferred earnings, we may reverse the transfer, debit your Connected Account where Stripe permits, deduct from future earnings or reserves, or charge your payment method.\n\n7.3 Delivery fees follow the fault rule: where the problem is attributable to the seller or provider, the customer can be refunded the delivery charge and AfroTransact keeps any seller-side delivery fee; where the customer changed their mind, the delivery charge is not refundable and the seller-side fee is returned in proportion. Details are in the Seller Fees Summary.\n\n## 8. Chargebacks, disputes, negative balances and recovery\n\n8.1 A chargeback or dispute is a reversal initiated by a customer''s bank or card network. Stripe and the networks apply their own rules and deadlines. Where a dispute relates to your order or booking, you are responsible for it, for the dispute fee, and for fines or penalties, as provided in the Stripe agreements and the Seller Terms.\n\n8.2 We may respond to a dispute on your behalf or ask you for evidence by a deadline. You must supply evidence (proof of delivery, tracking, communications, licences) on time. The bank or network decides. If a dispute is lost, the amount and fees are recovered from you.\n\n8.3 Negative balance. If your balance with us is negative (for example because refunds or chargebacks exceed your earnings) you owe the shortfall. We may recover it by deducting from future earnings, from reserves, by debiting your Connected Account where Stripe permits, or by charging your payment method on file, and we may stop payouts until it is repaid. Under the configuration of your Connected Account, Stripe bears the risk of your Connected Account''s negative balance as between Stripe and AfroTransact, but that does not release you from liability to Stripe under the Stripe agreements or to us under the Marketplace Terms. [[LEGAL: confirm how liability for negative balances is allocated between Stripe, AfroTransact and the seller and how to describe it]]\n\n8.4 Excessive disputes, refunds or fraud may lead to additional reserves, delayed payouts or termination.\n\n8.5 You must not fund, process or encourage transactions that you know to be unauthorised, card testing, laundering, or transaction laundering (processing payments for another business).\n\n## 9. Taxes and reporting\n\n9.1 Sales tax on marketplace sales is handled as in the Seller Terms. We and Stripe report payments to tax authorities where required. You must provide valid tax information. We may withhold payouts, or withhold tax, where tax information is missing or invalid.\n\n9.2 Your payout statements are not tax advice. You are responsible for your own income taxes.\n\n## 10. Sanctions, anti-money laundering and prohibited use\n\n10.1 You represent that you, your owners and your Practitioners are not subject to sanctions and are not located in or doing business with embargoed jurisdictions in breach of law. You will not use the Marketplace or your Connected Account for any activity that would violate sanctions, export-control, anti-money-laundering or anti-corruption laws.\n\n10.2 We and Stripe may screen you, block payments, freeze balances and report to authorities as required by law, without notice where the law forbids notice.\n\n10.3 We may refuse, reverse or hold any transaction that we believe violates law, card network rules or these terms.\n\n## 11. Data about payments\n\n11.1 Customers'' card numbers are collected by Stripe and are never stored by AfroTransact or shown to you. You receive only what you need to fulfil the order.\n\n11.2 Stripe processes your data and customers'' data as an independent controller for its own purposes (such as fraud prevention and legal compliance) and as our processor for others, under its own privacy policy and agreements. The Sub-processors list describes this. You must not attempt to obtain card data.\n\n## 12. Records\n\n12.1 We keep an immutable ledger of money movements and related records for at least the period required by law and by our policies [[LEGAL: confirm retention period; the Privacy Notice states 7 years for order records]]. You must keep your own records of sales, refunds, shipments, services, tax and payouts for at least seven (7) years or longer if the law requires, and provide them to us or Stripe on request.\n\n12.2 You authorise us to share information about you and your transactions with Stripe, banks, card networks, tax authorities and regulators as needed.\n\n## 13. Suspension, termination and survival\n\n13.1 We may close or restrict your ability to receive payments at any time under the Marketplace Terms or if Stripe so requires. Closing your Connected Account does not remove obligations for past transactions.\n\n13.2 After termination, remaining earnings are paid after the hold window and after deductions and any reserve under the Seller Terms.\n\n13.3 Sections on fees, refunds, chargebacks, negative balances, taxes, records and liability survive.\n\n## 14. Changes and contact\n\n14.1 We may change these Payment Terms as set out in the Seller Terms, and sooner where a change is required by Stripe, a card network, law or a regulator.\n\n14.2 Governing law and disputes are as in the Seller Terms: [[LEGAL: governing law and venue]]. Questions go to {legal}."}'::jsonb, 'New document. Explains payment processing for sellers and providers through Stripe Connect, the Stripe agreements you accept, verification, AfroTransact''s role, settlement and payout holds, fees, refunds, chargebacks, negative balances, sanctions and record retention.', now(), 'legal-loader', now(), null
 where not exists (select 1 from legal_version where doc_type = 'payments-terms' and (major, minor, patch) >= (1, 0, 0));
update legal_version set effective_date = '2026-12-01'::date, draft_banner = true,
       bodies = '{"en": "# Payment Terms for Sellers and Providers\n\n> DRAFT. This document was prepared with AI assistance and has not been reviewed by qualified counsel. It must be reviewed and approved by counsel before it is published or relied on. Text in double square brackets marked LEGAL is an open decision. Descriptions of third-party terms are summaries, not substitutes for the third party''s own text.\n\n## 1. Scope and order of priority\n\n1.1 These Payment Terms apply to everything about how money moves between customers, AfroTransact and you: payment acceptance, settlement, payouts, holds, reserves, refunds, chargebacks, fees and negative balances. They are part of the Seller Terms and the Service Provider Terms (together, the \"Marketplace Terms\"). For anything about money movement these Payment Terms control over the Marketplace Terms.\n\n1.2 \"We\" and \"AfroTransact\" mean [[LEGAL: contracting entity name]]. \"Stripe\" means the Stripe entity that provides payment services to you under the Stripe Agreements in section 3. \"Connected Account\" means your Stripe account created through AfroTransact.\n\n## 2. Who does what\n\n2.1 Payment processing is provided by Stripe. AfroTransact is a platform that uses Stripe Connect to accept customer payments for your sales and to send you your share. AfroTransact is not a bank. Your connected account is held with Stripe, not with AfroTransact.\n\n2.2 Customers pay AfroTransact''s checkout. We authorise the payment when an order or booking is placed and capture it as described in the Marketplace Terms (for products, when a package leaves for delivery; for services, a deposit when the booking is confirmed and the balance on completion). We then settle your share to your Connected Account after the hold window in section 6.\n\n2.3 [[LEGAL: money-transmission positioning. Confirm, with licensing counsel, whether AfroTransact''s receipt of customer funds and onward transfer to sellers and providers is structured as an agent-of-payee arrangement, a payment facilitator or marketplace exemption, or otherwise, and what state licences or registrations are or are not required. Do not publish this section until confirmed.]] We state that, as between you and us, we receive payments as the platform that facilitates your sale and settle your share through Stripe Connect.\n\n2.4 Where AfroTransact Retail sells as seller of record, the sale proceeds belong to AfroTransact Retail; there is no payout or transfer to it as if it were a third-party seller.\n\n2.5 We are not responsible for Stripe''s acts or omissions, but we will cooperate to resolve problems. Nothing in these Payment Terms makes Stripe a party to the Marketplace Terms except where those Terms say Stripe may rely on them.\n\n## 3. Stripe agreements you accept\n\n3.1 To receive payments you must have a Connected Account and accept Stripe''s terms. Your Connected Account is opened through embedded onboarding inside Seller Center or Provider Hub. By opening it you agree to the Stripe Connected Account Agreement, which incorporates the Stripe Services Agreement, and Stripe''s privacy policy [[LEGAL: confirm with counsel and Stripe''s current documents which Stripe service agreement applies to the connected account configuration used: Stripe verifies identity, Stripe is liable for negative balances of the connected account, the account pays Stripe''s fees, and there is no Stripe dashboard. Record the agreement version, date and jurisdiction version accepted by each account]]. Links will be shown at the point of acceptance:\n- Stripe Connected Account Agreement: https://stripe.com/legal/connect-account\n- Stripe Services Agreement: https://stripe.com/legal/ssa\n- Stripe Privacy Center: https://stripe.com/privacy\n\n3.2 The Stripe agreements are contracts between you and Stripe. We cannot change them or waive them. If they conflict with the Marketplace Terms about Stripe''s own rights and services, the Stripe agreements control as between you and Stripe. We may suspend your payouts or listings if Stripe closes, restricts or disables your Connected Account.\n\n3.3 You must comply with Stripe''s rules at all times, including its list of prohibited and restricted businesses (https://stripe.com/legal/restricted-businesses) and the card network rules that Stripe passes on. A business or product that Stripe prohibits cannot be sold or provided through AfroTransact, even if our own policies would otherwise allow it. Stripe may change its lists; the current list applies.\n\n3.4 Stripe may change its agreements and services. We are not liable for those changes, but we will tell you of changes to the Stripe requirements that we learn about and that affect you.\n\n## 4. Verification (KYC / KYB) and information\n\n4.1 You must complete Stripe''s identity and business verification before payouts are enabled. Stripe collects and verifies the information it needs (for example the legal name, tax identification number, date of birth, address, beneficial owners, and your payout bank account). You may be asked to supply more at any time, including when your volume grows.\n\n4.2 You must provide accurate, complete and up-to-date information to Stripe and to us, and respond by Stripe''s deadlines. If verification items become past due, Stripe may disable your charges or payouts, and we will pause payouts and hide your listings until they are resolved.\n\n4.3 Stripe collects the verification data and the payout bank account itself, through its own components in Seller Center or Provider Hub. AfroTransact does not store your full bank account number or full identity documents. We receive verification results, status flags and the last digits of your account for display and compliance. Tax identification numbers you give us during onboarding are stored encrypted and visible in masked form to reviewers.\n\n4.4 We may also run our own checks (including sanctions and adverse-media screening, document review and the two-step review in the Seller Terms). Stripe''s verification does not replace ours.\n\n4.5 You must tell us and Stripe promptly if your ownership, control, business model or category changes.\n\n## 5. Fees and who bears them\n\n5.1 AfroTransact fees (plan fee, commission, connection fee, labels, handling, delivery) are described in the Seller Terms, the Service Provider Terms and the Seller Fees Summary and are configured by Staff.\n\n5.2 Stripe''s processing fees on each payment and Stripe''s account and payout fees are charged under the Stripe agreements. As configured, your Connected Account bears Stripe''s account and payout fees, and the processing cost of each payment is allocated to the lines it relates to and shown on your statement. The exact allocation is shown in Seller Center.\n\n5.3 We may deduct our fees and amounts under section 8 from payments, transfers and payouts, from your Connected Account where Stripe permits, or invoice you.\n\n5.4 Currency. Customers pay in US dollars. Payouts are made in the currency and to the bank accounts Stripe supports for your Connected Account. Stripe may charge conversion fees.\n\n## 6. Settlement, hold window and payouts\n\n6.1 Authorisation and capture. For products, the payment is captured per shipment when the package is collected or leaves for delivery. For services, see the Service Provider Terms.\n\n6.2 Hold window. After delivery (or service completion) your net earnings for that order are held by AfroTransact for a hold window. The default is seven (7) days and Staff may configure it. The hold covers time for delivery problems, complaints, claims, refunds and chargebacks. [[LEGAL: confirm that the hold is not characterised as an unlawful retention or as custody of funds requiring a licence; confirm notice requirements for changing the period]]\n\n6.3 Transfer. After the hold window, we transfer the net amount to your Connected Account through Stripe, after deducting commission, fees, refunds, chargebacks, label charges, plan fees collected from earnings (if you chose that option) and other amounts under section 8. Transfers may be made in several parts.\n\n6.4 Payout. Your Connected Account is set to manual payouts. You request a payout from Seller Center or Provider Hub, subject to the minimum amount and frequency shown there (at the time of drafting, USD 25 and one request per day). Requests may be reviewed and approved. Approved payouts are sent by Stripe to your verified bank account and arrive according to Stripe''s and your bank''s timing.\n\n6.5 Holds. We may delay, pause or hold payouts or transfers, in whole or in part: while verification items are due or past due; during a suspension; during a claim, help request, dispute or chargeback; if we suspect fraud, sanctions risk or a breach; on Stripe''s request; and as required by law, a court or a regulator. A hold stays for as long as the reason exists, and we will tell you the reason when we lawfully can.\n\n6.6 Reserves. We may require a reserve as set out in the Seller Terms. Stripe may also establish a reserve or delay payouts under the Stripe Services Agreement, in which case Stripe''s reserve terms (which Stripe notifies to you) apply and we cannot override them.\n\n6.7 No interest. No interest accrues on any funds we hold.\n\n6.8 Incorrect or failed payouts. If a payout fails (for example because your bank details are wrong) it is returned to your balance, and you must correct your details. We are not liable for delays caused by Stripe, your bank or incorrect details.\n\n6.9 Statements. Seller Center and Provider Hub show your balance, pending earnings, holds, transfers, payouts and deductions, per order, and statement exports. You must review them and tell us of errors within [[LEGAL: error-notification period, for example 60 days]].\n\n## 7. Refunds\n\n7.1 Refunds to customers follow the Marketplace Terms and the Returns & Refunds policy. When a refund is made, the original payment method is credited for the refunded amount (and a part may be returned as AfroTransact store credit where Staff decide so in a support resolution).\n\n7.2 The refund is charged to the party responsible: to you when attributable to you, otherwise to AfroTransact. A refund reduces your earnings by the refunded item amount, adjusted by the commission or connection fee returned in proportion where the schedule says so. If the refund happens after we have transferred earnings, we may reverse the transfer, debit your Connected Account where Stripe permits, deduct from future earnings or reserves, or charge your payment method.\n\n7.3 Delivery fees follow the fault rule: where the problem is attributable to the seller or provider, the customer can be refunded the delivery charge and AfroTransact keeps any seller-side delivery fee; where the customer changed their mind, the delivery charge is not refundable and the seller-side fee is returned in proportion. Details are in the Seller Fees Summary.\n\n## 8. Chargebacks, disputes, negative balances and recovery\n\n8.1 A chargeback or dispute is a reversal initiated by a customer''s bank or card network. Stripe and the networks apply their own rules and deadlines. Where a dispute relates to your order or booking, you are responsible for it, for the dispute fee, and for fines or penalties, as provided in the Stripe agreements and the Seller Terms.\n\n8.2 We may respond to a dispute on your behalf or ask you for evidence by a deadline. You must supply evidence (proof of delivery, tracking, communications, licences) on time. The bank or network decides. If a dispute is lost, the amount and fees are recovered from you.\n\n8.3 Negative balance. If your balance with us is negative (for example because refunds or chargebacks exceed your earnings) you owe the shortfall. We may recover it by deducting from future earnings, from reserves, by debiting your Connected Account where Stripe permits, or by charging your payment method on file, and we may stop payouts until it is repaid. Under the configuration of your Connected Account, Stripe bears the risk of your Connected Account''s negative balance as between Stripe and AfroTransact, but that does not release you from liability to Stripe under the Stripe agreements or to us under the Marketplace Terms. [[LEGAL: confirm how liability for negative balances is allocated between Stripe, AfroTransact and the seller and how to describe it]]\n\n8.4 Excessive disputes, refunds or fraud may lead to additional reserves, delayed payouts or termination.\n\n8.5 You must not fund, process or encourage transactions that you know to be unauthorised, card testing, laundering, or transaction laundering (processing payments for another business).\n\n## 9. Taxes and reporting\n\n9.1 Sales tax on marketplace sales is handled as in the Seller Terms. We and Stripe report payments to tax authorities where required. You must provide valid tax information. We may withhold payouts, or withhold tax, where tax information is missing or invalid.\n\n9.2 Your payout statements are not tax advice. You are responsible for your own income taxes.\n\n## 10. Sanctions, anti-money laundering and prohibited use\n\n10.1 You represent that you, your owners and your Practitioners are not subject to sanctions and are not located in or doing business with embargoed jurisdictions in breach of law. You will not use the Marketplace or your Connected Account for any activity that would violate sanctions, export-control, anti-money-laundering or anti-corruption laws.\n\n10.2 We and Stripe may screen you, block payments, freeze balances and report to authorities as required by law, without notice where the law forbids notice.\n\n10.3 We may refuse, reverse or hold any transaction that we believe violates law, card network rules or these terms.\n\n## 11. Data about payments\n\n11.1 Customers'' card numbers are collected by Stripe and are never stored by AfroTransact or shown to you. You receive only what you need to fulfil the order.\n\n11.2 Stripe processes your data and customers'' data as an independent controller for its own purposes (such as fraud prevention and legal compliance) and as our processor for others, under its own privacy policy and agreements. The Sub-processors list describes this. You must not attempt to obtain card data.\n\n## 12. Records\n\n12.1 We keep an immutable ledger of money movements and related records for at least the period required by law and by our policies [[LEGAL: confirm retention period; the Privacy Notice states 7 years for order records]]. You must keep your own records of sales, refunds, shipments, services, tax and payouts for at least seven (7) years or longer if the law requires, and provide them to us or Stripe on request.\n\n12.2 You authorise us to share information about you and your transactions with Stripe, banks, card networks, tax authorities and regulators as needed.\n\n## 13. Suspension, termination and survival\n\n13.1 We may close or restrict your ability to receive payments at any time under the Marketplace Terms or if Stripe so requires. Closing your Connected Account does not remove obligations for past transactions.\n\n13.2 After termination, remaining earnings are paid after the hold window and after deductions and any reserve under the Seller Terms.\n\n13.3 Sections on fees, refunds, chargebacks, negative balances, taxes, records and liability survive.\n\n## 14. Changes and contact\n\n14.1 We may change these Payment Terms as set out in the Seller Terms, and sooner where a change is required by Stripe, a card network, law or a regulator.\n\n14.2 Governing law and disputes are as in the Seller Terms: [[LEGAL: governing law and venue]]. Questions go to {legal}."}'::jsonb, change_summary = 'New document. Explains payment processing for sellers and providers through Stripe Connect, the Stripe agreements you accept, verification, AfroTransact''s role, settlement and payout holds, fees, refunds, chargebacks, negative balances, sanctions and record retention.', created_at = now(), updated_at = now()
    where doc_type = 'payments-terms' and (major, minor, patch) = (1, 0, 0) and status = 'CURRENT'
      and created_by in ('seed', 'legal-loader') and updated_at = created_at;

-- subprocessors 1.0 (effective 2026-12-01; locales: en; draft banner on)
delete from legal_version where doc_type = 'subprocessors' and created_by = 'seed'
    and exists (select 1 from legal_version c where c.doc_type = 'subprocessors' and c.status = 'CURRENT'
                and c.created_by = 'seed' and c.updated_at = c.created_at and (c.major, c.minor, c.patch) > (1, 0, 0));
update legal_version set status = 'ARCHIVED', archived_at = now(), updated_at = now()
    where doc_type = 'subprocessors' and status = 'CURRENT' and (major, minor, patch) < (1, 0, 0);
insert into legal_version (id, doc_type, version, major, minor, patch, effective_date, status, draft_banner, bodies,
                           change_summary, created_at, created_by, updated_at, archived_at)
select 'ba967523-7b15-5b53-8011-a165405f9691'::uuid, 'subprocessors', '1.0', 1, 0, 0, '2026-12-01'::date, 'CURRENT', true,
       '{"en": "# Sub-processors and Service Providers\n\n> DRAFT. This document was prepared with AI assistance and has not been reviewed by qualified counsel. It was assembled from the platform''s configuration and documentation. Operations must confirm each entry, processing location and link against what is actually live before publication. Text in double square brackets marked LEGAL is an open decision.\n\n## 1. What this list is\n\n1.1 AfroTransact uses other companies to run parts of the Marketplace. This page lists them in plain language: who they are, what data they get, why, and where it is processed. It supports the Privacy Notice, the Data Processing Terms and the Seller Terms.\n\n1.2 \"Sub-processor\" means a company that processes personal data for us when we act as a processor or service provider for a seller or provider, or that we use to process personal data in running the Marketplace. Some companies below also act as independent controllers for their own purposes (for example Stripe, for fraud prevention and legal compliance). We say so where that applies.\n\n1.3 We only share the data a provider needs for the stated purpose, under written terms that limit its use and require appropriate security. We do not sell personal information for money.\n\n1.4 Only the providers marked \"in use\" are used in production. Providers marked \"failover\" or \"optional\" are configured as back-ups or are switched on only when enabled. Providers marked \"development only\" are not used with customer data in production.\n\n## 2. How to read the table\n\nEach entry shows the provider; what it does for us; the main categories of data it receives; where it processes data (the region our configuration uses, or \"United States / global\" where the provider operates globally and publishes its own locations); and a link to its privacy and terms pages. Links go to the provider''s own pages, which may change.\n\n## 3. Payments and identity verification\n\n| Provider | What it does | Data it receives | Where | Links |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Stripe, Inc. and its affiliates (in use) | Card and wallet payments, saved payment methods, refunds, disputes, Connect accounts for sellers and providers, identity and business verification (KYC/KYB), payouts, plan billing (subscriptions and invoices), fraud prevention. Stripe acts as a processor for payment processing and as an independent controller for fraud prevention, regulatory compliance and its own legal obligations. | Customers: name, email, billing details, card or bank details entered into Stripe''s fields (we never see full card numbers), order amount, device and fraud signals. Sellers and providers: legal name, address, date of birth, tax ID, bank account, identity documents, ownership details, business details. | United States and globally, per Stripe''s published locations | https://stripe.com/privacy; https://stripe.com/legal; https://stripe.com/legal/ssa; https://stripe.com/legal/connect-account |\n| Stripe Tax (optional) | Calculation of sales tax in addition to our in-house rules engine, if enabled. | Delivery address, product tax category, amounts. | As Stripe | As Stripe |\n\n## 4. Messaging: SMS, WhatsApp, email and push\n\n| Provider | What it does | Data it receives | Where | Links |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Twilio Inc. (in use) | Sends text messages: one-time codes, order, delivery, booking and security messages, and opt-in marketing; processes STOP/HELP replies and delivery receipts. | Mobile number, message content, delivery status, opt-out status. | United States and globally, per Twilio | https://www.twilio.com/en-us/legal/privacy; https://www.twilio.com/en-us/legal/tos; https://www.twilio.com/en-us/legal/messaging-policy; https://www.twilio.com/en-us/legal/aup |\n| Meta Platforms, Inc. (WhatsApp Business Platform) (optional; switched on per programme) | Sends WhatsApp messages from templates approved by Meta, only to people who opted in (for example delivery-run announcements). | WhatsApp phone number, message content from approved templates, delivery and read status, opt-in status. | United States and globally, per Meta | https://www.whatsapp.com/legal/business-terms; https://business.whatsapp.com/policy; https://www.facebook.com/privacy/policy |\n| Resend (in use) | Sends transactional email: order confirmations, shipping and account email, seller and provider notices, verification codes. | Email address, name, message content, delivery and bounce status. | United States (confirm region) | https://resend.com/legal/privacy-policy; https://resend.com/legal/terms-of-service; https://resend.com/legal/dpa [[LEGAL: confirm Resend''s current DPA link and sub-processors]] |\n| Amazon Web Services: Simple Email Service and End User Messaging (failover) | Back-up email and SMS delivery if the primary provider fails. | As for Twilio and Resend. | United States (configured region) | https://aws.amazon.com/privacy/; https://aws.amazon.com/service-terms/ |\n| SendGrid / Twilio SendGrid; Vonage (failover, configured but used only if enabled) | Back-up email and SMS delivery. | As for Resend and Twilio. | United States / global | https://www.twilio.com/en-us/legal/privacy; https://www.vonage.com/legal/privacy-policy/ |\n| Google Firebase Cloud Messaging and Apple Push Notification service (in use for app push) | Deliver push notifications to customer and driver mobile apps. | Device push token, notification content. | United States / global | https://policies.google.com/privacy; https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/ |\n\n## 5. Artificial intelligence\n\n| Provider | What it does | Data it receives | Where | Links |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenRouter, Inc. (in use) | Routes our requests to AI models for the shopping assistant (Lola), semantic search, listing writing and translation, triage and image moderation. | The text or images needed for the task. Personal details are redacted before model calls where our systems can do so. We configure it not to retain prompts. | United States; model hosts may be elsewhere | https://openrouter.ai/privacy; https://openrouter.ai/terms; https://openrouter.ai/data-processing-agreement |\n| Model providers reached through OpenRouter (in use: configured models include Google Gemini, OpenAI GPT and Anthropic Claude families) | Run the models. | The same task content, via OpenRouter. | As each provider publishes | https://policies.google.com/privacy; https://openai.com/policies/privacy-policy; https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy |\n\nConversations are not used by us to train third-party models. [[LEGAL: confirm this statement against the contracts and the provider data-retention settings actually applied, and align with the Privacy Notice]]\n\n## 6. Maps, geolocation and address verification\n\n| Provider | What it does | Data it receives | Where | Links |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Google (Maps Platform: Places, Geocoding, Routes) (in use) | Address autocomplete, place details, geocoding and drive-time routing for checkout, seller pickup locations, delivery coverage and routes. | Partial and full addresses typed into address fields, coordinates, origin and destination for routing. | United States / global | https://policies.google.com/privacy; https://cloud.google.com/maps-platform/terms |\n| OpenStreetMap Nominatim (fallback when no Google key is configured) | Address search fallback. | The address text typed. | Per OSM Foundation | https://osmfoundation.org/wiki/Privacy_Policy; https://operations.osmfoundation.org/policies/nominatim/ |\n| Smarty (optional) | US address deliverability checks. | Address. | United States | https://www.smarty.com/legal/privacy-policy [[LEGAL: confirm link]] |\n\n## 7. Hosting, storage, delivery network and databases\n\n| Provider | What it does | Data it receives | Where | Links |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cloud hosting: Google Cloud Platform, Amazon Web Services or Microsoft Azure (the platform can run on any; one is selected per environment) (in use) | Runs the application servers and object storage, including a locked bucket for seller verification documents. | All platform data, encrypted in transit and at rest where configured. | United States (production region: Virginia) [[LEGAL: confirm cloud and region]] | https://cloud.google.com/terms/data-processing-addendum; https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/gdpr-center/; https://www.microsoft.com/licensing/docs/view/Microsoft-Products-and-Services-Data-Protection-Addendum-DPA |\n| Layerbase (in use, per the current production design) | Hosts PostgreSQL and Valkey (cache) databases and the Meilisearch index. | Platform database content including account, order and booking data. | United States (Virginia) | https://layerbase.com [[LEGAL: confirm provider''s legal and privacy links]] |\n| Cloudflare (in use) | Content delivery network, DNS, web application protection, edge hosting of web apps and object storage (R2) for product images. | IP address, request metadata, images and files uploaded. | Global edge network | https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/; https://www.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-customer-dpa/ |\n| UploadThing (development and legacy; being replaced by Cloudflare R2) | File uploads for product images in non-production environments. | Uploaded images. | United States | https://uploadthing.com [[LEGAL: confirm link]] |\n\n## 8. Monitoring, error reporting and analytics\n\n| Provider | What it does | Data it receives | Where | Links |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Grafana Labs (Grafana Cloud) (in use) | Stores metrics, logs and traces to keep the platform running. | Technical telemetry. We avoid personal data in telemetry; some identifiers and IP addresses can appear in logs. | Per Grafana Cloud region | https://grafana.com/legal/privacy-policy/; https://grafana.com/legal/dpa/ |\n| Functional Software, Inc. (Sentry) (optional) | Collects browser and app error reports. | Error details, URL, device and browser information, user identifier. | United States | https://sentry.io/privacy/; https://sentry.io/legal/dpa/ |\n| Advertising and measurement (Google Ads conversion measurement; Meta pixel) (only if the visitor consents to advertising cookies; Global Privacy Control is honoured) | Measure ad conversions and show our ads on other sites. | Cookie identifiers, page and purchase events. | United States / global | https://policies.google.com/privacy; https://www.facebook.com/privacy/policy. The Cookie Notice lists each cookie. |\n| AfroTransact first-party analytics | De-identified page-performance measurement and delivery-run link attribution as aggregate counters. | Aggregated, no visitor identifier. | Our own infrastructure | Cookie Notice |\n\n## 9. Sign-in providers\n\n| Provider | What it does | Data it receives | Where | Links |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Google, Apple and Facebook (Meta) sign-in (each shown only if the customer chooses it) | Let a customer sign in with an existing account. | The identifiers and profile details the customer approves (name, email). | As the provider | https://policies.google.com/privacy; https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/; https://www.facebook.com/privacy/policy |\n\n## 10. Shipping carriers and delivery\n\n| Provider | What it does | Data it receives | Where | Links |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| UPS, USPS, FedEx and DHL Express (used for the carriers enabled in each market) | Provide rates, labels, tracking and delivery. Carriers act as independent businesses for delivery. | Recipient and sender name, address, phone, email where needed, package details, tracking events. | United States and international as the carrier operates | https://www.ups.com/us/en/support/shipping-support/legal-terms-conditions.page; https://www.usps.com/privacy/; https://www.fedex.com/en-us/privacy-policy.html; https://www.dhl.com/global-en/home/footer/global-privacy-notice.html [[LEGAL: confirm links]] |\n| Selling businesses (sellers, providers) and AfroTransact drivers | Not sub-processors. Sellers and providers receive the data needed to fulfil an order and act as independent controllers for it (see the Data Processing Terms). Drivers are engaged by or for AfroTransact and receive the delivery details for their route. | Name, delivery address, phone, delivery preferences, handover code check. | Not applicable | Seller Terms; Data Processing Terms |\n\n## 11. Product data services (no customer personal data)\n\nAfroTransact also uses services such as Open Food Facts and GS1 (product data), Pexels, Unsplash and Wikimedia (stock or sample images) to fill the catalogue. They receive product identifiers only, not personal data about customers, sellers or providers.\n\n## 12. Locations and international transfers\n\n12.1 Our production systems run in the United States. Some providers process data in other countries. Where the law requires a transfer mechanism, we rely on the mechanism described in the Data Processing Terms and the Privacy Notice [[LEGAL: transfer mechanism, for example Standard Contractual Clauses, UK addendum, Data Privacy Framework certification status]].\n\n## 13. Changes to this list and how you will be told\n\n13.1 We will update this page before we add or replace a sub-processor that processes personal data of sellers, providers or customers on our behalf, and we will show the date of the last change at the top of the page.\n\n13.2 For sellers and providers, we will give at least thirty (30) days'' notice [[LEGAL: confirm notice period and whether to offer a right to object and terminate]] of a new sub-processor, by email to the account owner and in Seller Center or Provider Hub. If you object on reasonable data-protection grounds in that period, tell us at {legal}; we will work with you in good faith and, if we cannot resolve the objection, you may close your account as the Data Processing Terms provide.\n\n13.3 We may add a provider immediately in an emergency (for example to replace one that has failed), and we will notify you promptly afterwards.\n\n13.4 Changes in the table of providers marked \"optional\" or \"failover\" count as changes once the provider is switched on in production.\n\n## 14. Contact\n\nQuestions about this list go to {privacy} or {legal}. Our address is {address}."}'::jsonb, 'New document. Plain-language list of the third-party providers that handle data for AfroTransact (payments, messaging, email, AI, maps and address, hosting, monitoring, carriers), what data they receive, why, where, and a commitment to give notice of changes.', now(), 'legal-loader', now(), null
 where not exists (select 1 from legal_version where doc_type = 'subprocessors' and (major, minor, patch) >= (1, 0, 0));
update legal_version set effective_date = '2026-12-01'::date, draft_banner = true,
       bodies = '{"en": "# Sub-processors and Service Providers\n\n> DRAFT. This document was prepared with AI assistance and has not been reviewed by qualified counsel. It was assembled from the platform''s configuration and documentation. Operations must confirm each entry, processing location and link against what is actually live before publication. Text in double square brackets marked LEGAL is an open decision.\n\n## 1. What this list is\n\n1.1 AfroTransact uses other companies to run parts of the Marketplace. This page lists them in plain language: who they are, what data they get, why, and where it is processed. It supports the Privacy Notice, the Data Processing Terms and the Seller Terms.\n\n1.2 \"Sub-processor\" means a company that processes personal data for us when we act as a processor or service provider for a seller or provider, or that we use to process personal data in running the Marketplace. Some companies below also act as independent controllers for their own purposes (for example Stripe, for fraud prevention and legal compliance). We say so where that applies.\n\n1.3 We only share the data a provider needs for the stated purpose, under written terms that limit its use and require appropriate security. We do not sell personal information for money.\n\n1.4 Only the providers marked \"in use\" are used in production. Providers marked \"failover\" or \"optional\" are configured as back-ups or are switched on only when enabled. Providers marked \"development only\" are not used with customer data in production.\n\n## 2. How to read the table\n\nEach entry shows the provider; what it does for us; the main categories of data it receives; where it processes data (the region our configuration uses, or \"United States / global\" where the provider operates globally and publishes its own locations); and a link to its privacy and terms pages. Links go to the provider''s own pages, which may change.\n\n## 3. Payments and identity verification\n\n| Provider | What it does | Data it receives | Where | Links |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Stripe, Inc. and its affiliates (in use) | Card and wallet payments, saved payment methods, refunds, disputes, Connect accounts for sellers and providers, identity and business verification (KYC/KYB), payouts, plan billing (subscriptions and invoices), fraud prevention. Stripe acts as a processor for payment processing and as an independent controller for fraud prevention, regulatory compliance and its own legal obligations. | Customers: name, email, billing details, card or bank details entered into Stripe''s fields (we never see full card numbers), order amount, device and fraud signals. Sellers and providers: legal name, address, date of birth, tax ID, bank account, identity documents, ownership details, business details. | United States and globally, per Stripe''s published locations | https://stripe.com/privacy; https://stripe.com/legal; https://stripe.com/legal/ssa; https://stripe.com/legal/connect-account |\n| Stripe Tax (optional) | Calculation of sales tax in addition to our in-house rules engine, if enabled. | Delivery address, product tax category, amounts. | As Stripe | As Stripe |\n\n## 4. Messaging: SMS, WhatsApp, email and push\n\n| Provider | What it does | Data it receives | Where | Links |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Twilio Inc. (in use) | Sends text messages: one-time codes, order, delivery, booking and security messages, and opt-in marketing; processes STOP/HELP replies and delivery receipts. | Mobile number, message content, delivery status, opt-out status. | United States and globally, per Twilio | https://www.twilio.com/en-us/legal/privacy; https://www.twilio.com/en-us/legal/tos; https://www.twilio.com/en-us/legal/messaging-policy; https://www.twilio.com/en-us/legal/aup |\n| Meta Platforms, Inc. (WhatsApp Business Platform) (optional; switched on per programme) | Sends WhatsApp messages from templates approved by Meta, only to people who opted in (for example delivery-run announcements). | WhatsApp phone number, message content from approved templates, delivery and read status, opt-in status. | United States and globally, per Meta | https://www.whatsapp.com/legal/business-terms; https://business.whatsapp.com/policy; https://www.facebook.com/privacy/policy |\n| Resend (in use) | Sends transactional email: order confirmations, shipping and account email, seller and provider notices, verification codes. | Email address, name, message content, delivery and bounce status. | United States (confirm region) | https://resend.com/legal/privacy-policy; https://resend.com/legal/terms-of-service; https://resend.com/legal/dpa [[LEGAL: confirm Resend''s current DPA link and sub-processors]] |\n| Amazon Web Services: Simple Email Service and End User Messaging (failover) | Back-up email and SMS delivery if the primary provider fails. | As for Twilio and Resend. | United States (configured region) | https://aws.amazon.com/privacy/; https://aws.amazon.com/service-terms/ |\n| SendGrid / Twilio SendGrid; Vonage (failover, configured but used only if enabled) | Back-up email and SMS delivery. | As for Resend and Twilio. | United States / global | https://www.twilio.com/en-us/legal/privacy; https://www.vonage.com/legal/privacy-policy/ |\n| Google Firebase Cloud Messaging and Apple Push Notification service (in use for app push) | Deliver push notifications to customer and driver mobile apps. | Device push token, notification content. | United States / global | https://policies.google.com/privacy; https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/ |\n\n## 5. Artificial intelligence\n\n| Provider | What it does | Data it receives | Where | Links |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenRouter, Inc. (in use) | Routes our requests to AI models for the shopping assistant (Lola), semantic search, listing writing and translation, triage and image moderation. | The text or images needed for the task. Personal details are redacted before model calls where our systems can do so. We configure it not to retain prompts. | United States; model hosts may be elsewhere | https://openrouter.ai/privacy; https://openrouter.ai/terms; https://openrouter.ai/data-processing-agreement |\n| Model providers reached through OpenRouter (in use: configured models include Google Gemini, OpenAI GPT and Anthropic Claude families) | Run the models. | The same task content, via OpenRouter. | As each provider publishes | https://policies.google.com/privacy; https://openai.com/policies/privacy-policy; https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy |\n\nConversations are not used by us to train third-party models. [[LEGAL: confirm this statement against the contracts and the provider data-retention settings actually applied, and align with the Privacy Notice]]\n\n## 6. Maps, geolocation and address verification\n\n| Provider | What it does | Data it receives | Where | Links |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Google (Maps Platform: Places, Geocoding, Routes) (in use) | Address autocomplete, place details, geocoding and drive-time routing for checkout, seller pickup locations, delivery coverage and routes. | Partial and full addresses typed into address fields, coordinates, origin and destination for routing. | United States / global | https://policies.google.com/privacy; https://cloud.google.com/maps-platform/terms |\n| OpenStreetMap Nominatim (fallback when no Google key is configured) | Address search fallback. | The address text typed. | Per OSM Foundation | https://osmfoundation.org/wiki/Privacy_Policy; https://operations.osmfoundation.org/policies/nominatim/ |\n| Smarty (optional) | US address deliverability checks. | Address. | United States | https://www.smarty.com/legal/privacy-policy [[LEGAL: confirm link]] |\n\n## 7. Hosting, storage, delivery network and databases\n\n| Provider | What it does | Data it receives | Where | Links |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cloud hosting: Google Cloud Platform, Amazon Web Services or Microsoft Azure (the platform can run on any; one is selected per environment) (in use) | Runs the application servers and object storage, including a locked bucket for seller verification documents. | All platform data, encrypted in transit and at rest where configured. | United States (production region: Virginia) [[LEGAL: confirm cloud and region]] | https://cloud.google.com/terms/data-processing-addendum; https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/gdpr-center/; https://www.microsoft.com/licensing/docs/view/Microsoft-Products-and-Services-Data-Protection-Addendum-DPA |\n| Layerbase (in use, per the current production design) | Hosts PostgreSQL and Valkey (cache) databases and the Meilisearch index. | Platform database content including account, order and booking data. | United States (Virginia) | https://layerbase.com [[LEGAL: confirm provider''s legal and privacy links]] |\n| Cloudflare (in use) | Content delivery network, DNS, web application protection, edge hosting of web apps and object storage (R2) for product images. | IP address, request metadata, images and files uploaded. | Global edge network | https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/; https://www.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-customer-dpa/ |\n| UploadThing (development and legacy; being replaced by Cloudflare R2) | File uploads for product images in non-production environments. | Uploaded images. | United States | https://uploadthing.com [[LEGAL: confirm link]] |\n\n## 8. Monitoring, error reporting and analytics\n\n| Provider | What it does | Data it receives | Where | Links |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Grafana Labs (Grafana Cloud) (in use) | Stores metrics, logs and traces to keep the platform running. | Technical telemetry. We avoid personal data in telemetry; some identifiers and IP addresses can appear in logs. | Per Grafana Cloud region | https://grafana.com/legal/privacy-policy/; https://grafana.com/legal/dpa/ |\n| Functional Software, Inc. (Sentry) (optional) | Collects browser and app error reports. | Error details, URL, device and browser information, user identifier. | United States | https://sentry.io/privacy/; https://sentry.io/legal/dpa/ |\n| Advertising and measurement (Google Ads conversion measurement; Meta pixel) (only if the visitor consents to advertising cookies; Global Privacy Control is honoured) | Measure ad conversions and show our ads on other sites. | Cookie identifiers, page and purchase events. | United States / global | https://policies.google.com/privacy; https://www.facebook.com/privacy/policy. The Cookie Notice lists each cookie. |\n| AfroTransact first-party analytics | De-identified page-performance measurement and delivery-run link attribution as aggregate counters. | Aggregated, no visitor identifier. | Our own infrastructure | Cookie Notice |\n\n## 9. Sign-in providers\n\n| Provider | What it does | Data it receives | Where | Links |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Google, Apple and Facebook (Meta) sign-in (each shown only if the customer chooses it) | Let a customer sign in with an existing account. | The identifiers and profile details the customer approves (name, email). | As the provider | https://policies.google.com/privacy; https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/; https://www.facebook.com/privacy/policy |\n\n## 10. Shipping carriers and delivery\n\n| Provider | What it does | Data it receives | Where | Links |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| UPS, USPS, FedEx and DHL Express (used for the carriers enabled in each market) | Provide rates, labels, tracking and delivery. Carriers act as independent businesses for delivery. | Recipient and sender name, address, phone, email where needed, package details, tracking events. | United States and international as the carrier operates | https://www.ups.com/us/en/support/shipping-support/legal-terms-conditions.page; https://www.usps.com/privacy/; https://www.fedex.com/en-us/privacy-policy.html; https://www.dhl.com/global-en/home/footer/global-privacy-notice.html [[LEGAL: confirm links]] |\n| Selling businesses (sellers, providers) and AfroTransact drivers | Not sub-processors. Sellers and providers receive the data needed to fulfil an order and act as independent controllers for it (see the Data Processing Terms). Drivers are engaged by or for AfroTransact and receive the delivery details for their route. | Name, delivery address, phone, delivery preferences, handover code check. | Not applicable | Seller Terms; Data Processing Terms |\n\n## 11. Product data services (no customer personal data)\n\nAfroTransact also uses services such as Open Food Facts and GS1 (product data), Pexels, Unsplash and Wikimedia (stock or sample images) to fill the catalogue. They receive product identifiers only, not personal data about customers, sellers or providers.\n\n## 12. Locations and international transfers\n\n12.1 Our production systems run in the United States. Some providers process data in other countries. Where the law requires a transfer mechanism, we rely on the mechanism described in the Data Processing Terms and the Privacy Notice [[LEGAL: transfer mechanism, for example Standard Contractual Clauses, UK addendum, Data Privacy Framework certification status]].\n\n## 13. Changes to this list and how you will be told\n\n13.1 We will update this page before we add or replace a sub-processor that processes personal data of sellers, providers or customers on our behalf, and we will show the date of the last change at the top of the page.\n\n13.2 For sellers and providers, we will give at least thirty (30) days'' notice [[LEGAL: confirm notice period and whether to offer a right to object and terminate]] of a new sub-processor, by email to the account owner and in Seller Center or Provider Hub. If you object on reasonable data-protection grounds in that period, tell us at {legal}; we will work with you in good faith and, if we cannot resolve the objection, you may close your account as the Data Processing Terms provide.\n\n13.3 We may add a provider immediately in an emergency (for example to replace one that has failed), and we will notify you promptly afterwards.\n\n13.4 Changes in the table of providers marked \"optional\" or \"failover\" count as changes once the provider is switched on in production.\n\n## 14. Contact\n\nQuestions about this list go to {privacy} or {legal}. Our address is {address}."}'::jsonb, change_summary = 'New document. Plain-language list of the third-party providers that handle data for AfroTransact (payments, messaging, email, AI, maps and address, hosting, monitoring, carriers), what data they receive, why, where, and a commitment to give notice of changes.', created_at = now(), updated_at = now()
    where doc_type = 'subprocessors' and (major, minor, patch) = (1, 0, 0) and status = 'CURRENT'
      and created_by in ('seed', 'legal-loader') and updated_at = created_at;

-- data-processing 1.0 (effective 2026-12-01; locales: en; draft banner on)
delete from legal_version where doc_type = 'data-processing' and created_by = 'seed'
    and exists (select 1 from legal_version c where c.doc_type = 'data-processing' and c.status = 'CURRENT'
                and c.created_by = 'seed' and c.updated_at = c.created_at and (c.major, c.minor, c.patch) > (1, 0, 0));
update legal_version set status = 'ARCHIVED', archived_at = now(), updated_at = now()
    where doc_type = 'data-processing' and status = 'CURRENT' and (major, minor, patch) < (1, 0, 0);
insert into legal_version (id, doc_type, version, major, minor, patch, effective_date, status, draft_banner, bodies,
                           change_summary, created_at, created_by, updated_at, archived_at)
select '61d72882-a699-5bec-a816-545300c222db'::uuid, 'data-processing', '1.0', 1, 0, 0, '2026-12-01'::date, 'CURRENT', true,
       '{"en": "# Data Processing Terms\n\n> DRAFT. This document was prepared with AI assistance and has not been reviewed by qualified counsel. It must be reviewed and approved by counsel before it is published or relied on, including against the privacy laws that apply in each live market. Text in double square brackets marked LEGAL is an open decision.\n\n## 1. Purpose, scope and order of priority\n\n1.1 These Data Processing Terms (\"DP Terms\") form part of the Seller Terms and the Service Provider Terms (the \"Marketplace Terms\") between [[LEGAL: contracting entity name]] (\"AfroTransact\") and the seller or provider (\"you\"). They describe how each of us handles personal data connected with orders and bookings.\n\n1.2 If these DP Terms conflict with the Marketplace Terms about personal data, these DP Terms control. The Payment Terms control for payment card and bank data held by Stripe.\n\n1.3 Definitions. \"Applicable Data Protection Law\" means the privacy and data protection laws that apply to the processing, which may include U.S. state consumer privacy laws (for example the California Consumer Privacy Act as amended), federal laws, and, where they apply, the UK GDPR and EU GDPR [[LEGAL: list the laws that apply in each live market]]. \"Personal Data\", \"controller\", \"processor\", \"business\", \"service provider\", \"sell\", \"share\", \"process\" and \"security incident\" have the meanings given by those laws. \"Customer Data\" is personal data about customers, recipients and other individuals that you receive through the Marketplace. \"Order Data\" is the part of Customer Data needed to fulfil an order or booking. \"Sub-processor\" is as defined on the Sub-processors list.\n\n## 2. Roles of the parties\n\n2.1 Independent controllers for fulfilment data. For Order Data (such as name, delivery address, phone number, email address if shared, order contents, delivery preferences, appointment details and service notes), you are an independent controller (or a \"business\" under U.S. law) for the purposes of fulfilling the order or booking, handling returns, claims and support for it, meeting your legal, tax and accounting duties, and defending legal claims. AfroTransact is a separate and independent controller for the data it collects to operate the Marketplace. Neither of us is the other''s processor for Order Data.\n\n2.2 AfroTransact as processor. Where AfroTransact processes personal data on your behalf for your own purposes (for example personal data of your team members that you add to Seller Center or Provider Hub, or data you ask us to store in your catalogue or notes), AfroTransact acts as your processor (or \"service provider\") and sections 4 to 12 apply to that processing. The subject matter, duration, nature, purpose, data types and data subjects are described in Annex A.\n\n2.3 AfroTransact as controller of your data. AfroTransact also processes data about you and your team (for onboarding, verification, risk, payments, support and legal compliance) as a controller, as described in the Privacy Notice.\n\n2.4 Each party complies with Applicable Data Protection Law for its own processing. [[LEGAL: confirm that independent-controller characterisation is appropriate for each fulfilment scenario, including service providers handling sensitive (for example health-related) data and sellers using their own couriers.]]\n\n## 3. What you may do with Customer Data\n\n3.1 You must use Customer Data only to: fulfil the order or booking; communicate with the customer about it through the Marketplace; provide customer support and warranty service; comply with law; prevent fraud; and establish, exercise or defend legal claims.\n\n3.2 You must not: sell or share Customer Data (as those terms are defined by Applicable Data Protection Law), including for cross-context behavioural advertising; use it for your own marketing, promotions, newsletters or lists, or add the customer to any list, unless the customer has given you a separate lawful opt-in outside the order; combine it with data from other sources to profile the customer; contact the customer by text, WhatsApp, phone or email outside the Marketplace except as needed to fulfil the order, and never for marketing without valid consent; transfer it to anyone other than your own carriers, couriers and service providers who need it to fulfil the order and who are bound by confidentiality; or attempt to re-identify de-identified data.\n\n3.3 You are responsible for your team members, couriers, carriers and service providers who receive Customer Data, and for their compliance.\n\n3.4 You must give customers any notice that the law requires of you as a controller (for example a privacy notice available on request) and honour their rights as set out in section 8.\n\n3.5 Sensitive data. If your Services involve sensitive personal data (for example health, religion, sex life, biometric or precise location data), you must comply with the extra legal requirements for it, obtain any consent required, and limit collection to what is necessary. [[LEGAL: confirm whether AfroTransact will permit health-related services, and if so what restrictions apply.]]\n\n## 4. Security\n\n4.1 Each party must implement appropriate technical and organisational measures to protect personal data against unauthorised or unlawful processing, loss, destruction, alteration and disclosure, proportionate to the risk. At a minimum you must: restrict access to people who need it, with unique accounts and multi-factor authentication where offered; keep devices and software up to date; use encryption for data in transit and for devices that store Customer Data; keep Customer Data out of public spreadsheets, shared inboxes and personal accounts; train staff on privacy and security; protect printed shipping labels and packing slips; and securely dispose of data.\n\n4.2 AfroTransact maintains the measures described in Annex B for the data it processes. We may update them provided the level of protection is not materially reduced.\n\n4.3 Handover codes. You must keep customers'' handover codes and delivery proof confidential and use them only for the delivery.\n\n4.4 You must not copy Customer Data from Seller Center or Provider Hub into other systems except as needed to fulfil orders, and you must protect any copy.\n\n## 5. Confidentiality\n\nEach person you authorise to access Customer Data must be under a duty of confidentiality. Access must end when they leave or change role.\n\n## 6. Security incidents\n\n6.1 If you become aware of a security incident that affects Customer Data (including loss of a device, compromise of an account, accidental disclosure or unauthorised access), you must notify us without undue delay and in any event within seventy-two (72) hours [[LEGAL: confirm notice period; consider 24 to 72 hours]] at {privacy} or {legal}, give us the information in 6.3 as it becomes available, take steps to contain and remedy it, and cooperate with us.\n\n6.2 If we become aware of a security incident affecting personal data that we process on your behalf, we will notify you without undue delay and, where feasible, within seventy-two (72) hours of becoming aware [[LEGAL: confirm notice period]]. Where AfroTransact is a controller of the affected data, we notify authorities and individuals as the law requires and will tell you if the incident affects your customers'' orders.\n\n6.3 The notice should describe what happened, the categories and approximate number of individuals and records, the likely consequences, the measures taken and a contact point.\n\n6.4 Neither party will inform affected customers or authorities about an incident in a way that names the other, unless the law requires it or the other agrees (not unreasonably withheld).\n\n6.5 Each controller is responsible for its own regulatory notifications. You bear the cost of incidents in your systems or caused by your people.\n\n## 7. Sub-processors\n\n7.1 For processing we do on your behalf, you give general authorisation for us to use the sub-processors listed on the Sub-processors list.\n\n7.2 We will give you notice of new sub-processors as stated in the Sub-processors list and you may object on reasonable data-protection grounds in the period stated there. If we cannot resolve the objection, you may terminate the affected service by closing your account before the new sub-processor starts, and we will not process your data with that sub-processor in the meantime if that is practicable [[LEGAL: confirm remedy]].\n\n7.3 We impose on sub-processors written terms that provide at least equivalent protection for the data and remain responsible for their performance to the extent the law requires.\n\n7.4 Your own service providers are your responsibility.\n\n## 8. Individuals'' rights and requests\n\n8.1 If a customer asks you to access, correct, delete or restrict their data, opt out of sale or sharing, or object to processing, you must handle the request according to Applicable Data Protection Law for the data you control, within the legal time limits, and without charge where the law requires.\n\n8.2 If you receive a request that is really for AfroTransact (for example about their account or the Marketplace), forward it to {privacy} within five (5) business days.\n\n8.3 We will help you with requests about data we process on your behalf, taking into account its nature, and may charge reasonable fees for requests that are manifestly excessive or complex [[LEGAL: confirm cost allocation]].\n\n8.4 Customers can also make requests to us under the Privacy Notice and Your Privacy Choices page. We may tell you of requests that affect Order Data you hold, for example a deletion request, and you must comply to the extent the law applies to you.\n\n## 9. Assistance, assessments and records\n\n9.1 Each party will give reasonable assistance to the other in meeting its obligations about security, incidents, impact assessments and prior consultations, as relevant to the processing.\n\n9.2 Each party will keep records of its processing as the law requires.\n\n## 10. Return and deletion\n\n10.1 You must delete or irreversibly de-identify Customer Data when it is no longer needed for the purposes in section 3, and in any event at the end of the period your law requires you to keep records (for orders, usually the shorter of what you need and the record-retention period in the Seller Terms), unless the law requires longer. Do not keep Customer Data merely because you might want to contact the customer again.\n\n10.2 When your account ends, you must stop using Customer Data except as the law requires you to keep it, delete what remains within [[LEGAL: deletion deadline after account closure, for example 90 days]], and on request confirm in writing that you have done so.\n\n10.3 For data we process on your behalf, at your request or at the end of the service we will return or delete it within [[LEGAL: deletion deadline, for example 30 to 90 days]], except where the law requires us to keep it, and backups are deleted in the ordinary cycle. We will give you an export of your catalogue, order and earnings statements for a reasonable period after termination.\n\n10.4 We may keep Order Data and records for the period the Privacy Notice states (at the time of drafting, seven years for order records) for tax, accounting, fraud and legal reasons, as a controller.\n\n## 11. International transfers\n\n11.1 Customer Data is processed in the United States. If a transfer of personal data is subject to a legal restriction (for example transfers from the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom or Switzerland to a country without an adequacy decision), the parties will rely on [[LEGAL: transfer mechanism: Standard Contractual Clauses (EU 2021/914) modules, UK International Data Transfer Addendum, Swiss addendum, and/or participation in the EU–U.S. Data Privacy Framework. Insert the clauses by reference, select modules (controller to controller; controller to processor), specify governing law, supervisory authority, annex details and the docking clause. Do not publish until chosen.]]\n\n11.2 You must not transfer Customer Data to another country in a way that breaches Applicable Data Protection Law, and must ensure the safeguards the law requires.\n\n## 12. Audit and compliance\n\n12.1 Each party must be able to show compliance with these DP Terms. On reasonable written request (not more than once a year, unless there has been an incident or a regulator requires it) you will give us information about your handling of Customer Data, and we will give you information about the data we process for you (for example our current security summary and third-party attestations or audit reports we hold). Audits, if needed, are by an independent auditor under confidentiality, during business hours and with reasonable notice. [[LEGAL: confirm the audit scope and costs]]\n\n12.2 If we reasonably believe you are using Customer Data in breach of these DP Terms, we may suspend your access to Customer Data, require remediation and, if you do not remedy, close your account.\n\n## 13. Liability\n\n13.1 The limits and exclusions of liability in the Seller Terms apply to these DP Terms. Each party is responsible to the other for breaches of these DP Terms and to individuals as the law provides. You indemnify AfroTransact as set out in the Seller Terms for claims arising from your handling of Customer Data. [[LEGAL: confirm whether data-protection liability should be uncapped or have a super-cap]]\n\n## 14. Changes, term and contact\n\n14.1 These DP Terms run for as long as you have an account and for as long as you hold Customer Data. We may update them under the change provisions of the Seller Terms, and sooner if required by law or by a regulator.\n\n14.2 Contact for privacy matters: {privacy}. Legal notices: {legal}. Address: {address}.\n\n## Annex A. Description of processing when AfroTransact is your processor\n\n| Item | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Subject matter and nature | Hosting and operating Seller Center or Provider Hub for you; storing your team members'' account details, your catalogue and notes; sending messages you ask us to send through the Marketplace. |\n| Purpose | To provide the Marketplace tools to you. |\n| Duration | The life of your account plus the deletion period in section 10. |\n| Types of data | Team member names, work email, phone, role and activity logs; your catalogue content; notes and messages you enter. |\n| Data subjects | Your team members and contractors; individuals named in your notes. |\n| Sub-processors | As on the Sub-processors list. |\n\n## Annex B. AfroTransact security measures (summary)\n\nThe following describes controls at the time of drafting, derived from the platform''s documentation. Operations must confirm each before publication [[LEGAL: operations to verify each statement]].\n- Access control: role-based access, with separation of duties for seller review (two different reviewers) and masked display of sensitive fields; reviewer views of documents and unmasked identifiers are logged.\n- Authentication: central identity service, multi-factor options, step-up for sensitive administrative actions.\n- Encryption: encryption in transit; sensitive identifiers (such as tax identification numbers) stored as masked values with field-level encryption; keys managed separately and rotated.\n- Payment data: card and bank details are collected by Stripe and not stored by AfroTransact.\n- Documents: verification documents are held in a restricted bucket and accessed through signed, logged links.\n- Audit: an append-only, hash-chained audit log of staff decisions.\n- Network and application security: vulnerability scanning, secret scanning, least-privilege service credentials, secrets held in a cloud secret store.\n- Monitoring and incident response: metrics, logs and alerts, with an on-call process.\n- Resilience: backups and restore procedures [[LEGAL: operations to describe backup frequency and testing]].\n- Data minimisation: telemetry is scrubbed of personal data where practicable; handover codes are not stored in clear text in delivery event logs."}'::jsonb, 'New document. DPA-style terms for sellers and providers that handle customer data: roles, permitted use, security, incident notice, assistance with rights requests, deletion, sub-processors, international transfers (placeholder) and audit.', now(), 'legal-loader', now(), null
 where not exists (select 1 from legal_version where doc_type = 'data-processing' and (major, minor, patch) >= (1, 0, 0));
update legal_version set effective_date = '2026-12-01'::date, draft_banner = true,
       bodies = '{"en": "# Data Processing Terms\n\n> DRAFT. This document was prepared with AI assistance and has not been reviewed by qualified counsel. It must be reviewed and approved by counsel before it is published or relied on, including against the privacy laws that apply in each live market. Text in double square brackets marked LEGAL is an open decision.\n\n## 1. Purpose, scope and order of priority\n\n1.1 These Data Processing Terms (\"DP Terms\") form part of the Seller Terms and the Service Provider Terms (the \"Marketplace Terms\") between [[LEGAL: contracting entity name]] (\"AfroTransact\") and the seller or provider (\"you\"). They describe how each of us handles personal data connected with orders and bookings.\n\n1.2 If these DP Terms conflict with the Marketplace Terms about personal data, these DP Terms control. The Payment Terms control for payment card and bank data held by Stripe.\n\n1.3 Definitions. \"Applicable Data Protection Law\" means the privacy and data protection laws that apply to the processing, which may include U.S. state consumer privacy laws (for example the California Consumer Privacy Act as amended), federal laws, and, where they apply, the UK GDPR and EU GDPR [[LEGAL: list the laws that apply in each live market]]. \"Personal Data\", \"controller\", \"processor\", \"business\", \"service provider\", \"sell\", \"share\", \"process\" and \"security incident\" have the meanings given by those laws. \"Customer Data\" is personal data about customers, recipients and other individuals that you receive through the Marketplace. \"Order Data\" is the part of Customer Data needed to fulfil an order or booking. \"Sub-processor\" is as defined on the Sub-processors list.\n\n## 2. Roles of the parties\n\n2.1 Independent controllers for fulfilment data. For Order Data (such as name, delivery address, phone number, email address if shared, order contents, delivery preferences, appointment details and service notes), you are an independent controller (or a \"business\" under U.S. law) for the purposes of fulfilling the order or booking, handling returns, claims and support for it, meeting your legal, tax and accounting duties, and defending legal claims. AfroTransact is a separate and independent controller for the data it collects to operate the Marketplace. Neither of us is the other''s processor for Order Data.\n\n2.2 AfroTransact as processor. Where AfroTransact processes personal data on your behalf for your own purposes (for example personal data of your team members that you add to Seller Center or Provider Hub, or data you ask us to store in your catalogue or notes), AfroTransact acts as your processor (or \"service provider\") and sections 4 to 12 apply to that processing. The subject matter, duration, nature, purpose, data types and data subjects are described in Annex A.\n\n2.3 AfroTransact as controller of your data. AfroTransact also processes data about you and your team (for onboarding, verification, risk, payments, support and legal compliance) as a controller, as described in the Privacy Notice.\n\n2.4 Each party complies with Applicable Data Protection Law for its own processing. [[LEGAL: confirm that independent-controller characterisation is appropriate for each fulfilment scenario, including service providers handling sensitive (for example health-related) data and sellers using their own couriers.]]\n\n## 3. What you may do with Customer Data\n\n3.1 You must use Customer Data only to: fulfil the order or booking; communicate with the customer about it through the Marketplace; provide customer support and warranty service; comply with law; prevent fraud; and establish, exercise or defend legal claims.\n\n3.2 You must not: sell or share Customer Data (as those terms are defined by Applicable Data Protection Law), including for cross-context behavioural advertising; use it for your own marketing, promotions, newsletters or lists, or add the customer to any list, unless the customer has given you a separate lawful opt-in outside the order; combine it with data from other sources to profile the customer; contact the customer by text, WhatsApp, phone or email outside the Marketplace except as needed to fulfil the order, and never for marketing without valid consent; transfer it to anyone other than your own carriers, couriers and service providers who need it to fulfil the order and who are bound by confidentiality; or attempt to re-identify de-identified data.\n\n3.3 You are responsible for your team members, couriers, carriers and service providers who receive Customer Data, and for their compliance.\n\n3.4 You must give customers any notice that the law requires of you as a controller (for example a privacy notice available on request) and honour their rights as set out in section 8.\n\n3.5 Sensitive data. If your Services involve sensitive personal data (for example health, religion, sex life, biometric or precise location data), you must comply with the extra legal requirements for it, obtain any consent required, and limit collection to what is necessary. [[LEGAL: confirm whether AfroTransact will permit health-related services, and if so what restrictions apply.]]\n\n## 4. Security\n\n4.1 Each party must implement appropriate technical and organisational measures to protect personal data against unauthorised or unlawful processing, loss, destruction, alteration and disclosure, proportionate to the risk. At a minimum you must: restrict access to people who need it, with unique accounts and multi-factor authentication where offered; keep devices and software up to date; use encryption for data in transit and for devices that store Customer Data; keep Customer Data out of public spreadsheets, shared inboxes and personal accounts; train staff on privacy and security; protect printed shipping labels and packing slips; and securely dispose of data.\n\n4.2 AfroTransact maintains the measures described in Annex B for the data it processes. We may update them provided the level of protection is not materially reduced.\n\n4.3 Handover codes. You must keep customers'' handover codes and delivery proof confidential and use them only for the delivery.\n\n4.4 You must not copy Customer Data from Seller Center or Provider Hub into other systems except as needed to fulfil orders, and you must protect any copy.\n\n## 5. Confidentiality\n\nEach person you authorise to access Customer Data must be under a duty of confidentiality. Access must end when they leave or change role.\n\n## 6. Security incidents\n\n6.1 If you become aware of a security incident that affects Customer Data (including loss of a device, compromise of an account, accidental disclosure or unauthorised access), you must notify us without undue delay and in any event within seventy-two (72) hours [[LEGAL: confirm notice period; consider 24 to 72 hours]] at {privacy} or {legal}, give us the information in 6.3 as it becomes available, take steps to contain and remedy it, and cooperate with us.\n\n6.2 If we become aware of a security incident affecting personal data that we process on your behalf, we will notify you without undue delay and, where feasible, within seventy-two (72) hours of becoming aware [[LEGAL: confirm notice period]]. Where AfroTransact is a controller of the affected data, we notify authorities and individuals as the law requires and will tell you if the incident affects your customers'' orders.\n\n6.3 The notice should describe what happened, the categories and approximate number of individuals and records, the likely consequences, the measures taken and a contact point.\n\n6.4 Neither party will inform affected customers or authorities about an incident in a way that names the other, unless the law requires it or the other agrees (not unreasonably withheld).\n\n6.5 Each controller is responsible for its own regulatory notifications. You bear the cost of incidents in your systems or caused by your people.\n\n## 7. Sub-processors\n\n7.1 For processing we do on your behalf, you give general authorisation for us to use the sub-processors listed on the Sub-processors list.\n\n7.2 We will give you notice of new sub-processors as stated in the Sub-processors list and you may object on reasonable data-protection grounds in the period stated there. If we cannot resolve the objection, you may terminate the affected service by closing your account before the new sub-processor starts, and we will not process your data with that sub-processor in the meantime if that is practicable [[LEGAL: confirm remedy]].\n\n7.3 We impose on sub-processors written terms that provide at least equivalent protection for the data and remain responsible for their performance to the extent the law requires.\n\n7.4 Your own service providers are your responsibility.\n\n## 8. Individuals'' rights and requests\n\n8.1 If a customer asks you to access, correct, delete or restrict their data, opt out of sale or sharing, or object to processing, you must handle the request according to Applicable Data Protection Law for the data you control, within the legal time limits, and without charge where the law requires.\n\n8.2 If you receive a request that is really for AfroTransact (for example about their account or the Marketplace), forward it to {privacy} within five (5) business days.\n\n8.3 We will help you with requests about data we process on your behalf, taking into account its nature, and may charge reasonable fees for requests that are manifestly excessive or complex [[LEGAL: confirm cost allocation]].\n\n8.4 Customers can also make requests to us under the Privacy Notice and Your Privacy Choices page. We may tell you of requests that affect Order Data you hold, for example a deletion request, and you must comply to the extent the law applies to you.\n\n## 9. Assistance, assessments and records\n\n9.1 Each party will give reasonable assistance to the other in meeting its obligations about security, incidents, impact assessments and prior consultations, as relevant to the processing.\n\n9.2 Each party will keep records of its processing as the law requires.\n\n## 10. Return and deletion\n\n10.1 You must delete or irreversibly de-identify Customer Data when it is no longer needed for the purposes in section 3, and in any event at the end of the period your law requires you to keep records (for orders, usually the shorter of what you need and the record-retention period in the Seller Terms), unless the law requires longer. Do not keep Customer Data merely because you might want to contact the customer again.\n\n10.2 When your account ends, you must stop using Customer Data except as the law requires you to keep it, delete what remains within [[LEGAL: deletion deadline after account closure, for example 90 days]], and on request confirm in writing that you have done so.\n\n10.3 For data we process on your behalf, at your request or at the end of the service we will return or delete it within [[LEGAL: deletion deadline, for example 30 to 90 days]], except where the law requires us to keep it, and backups are deleted in the ordinary cycle. We will give you an export of your catalogue, order and earnings statements for a reasonable period after termination.\n\n10.4 We may keep Order Data and records for the period the Privacy Notice states (at the time of drafting, seven years for order records) for tax, accounting, fraud and legal reasons, as a controller.\n\n## 11. International transfers\n\n11.1 Customer Data is processed in the United States. If a transfer of personal data is subject to a legal restriction (for example transfers from the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom or Switzerland to a country without an adequacy decision), the parties will rely on [[LEGAL: transfer mechanism: Standard Contractual Clauses (EU 2021/914) modules, UK International Data Transfer Addendum, Swiss addendum, and/or participation in the EU–U.S. Data Privacy Framework. Insert the clauses by reference, select modules (controller to controller; controller to processor), specify governing law, supervisory authority, annex details and the docking clause. Do not publish until chosen.]]\n\n11.2 You must not transfer Customer Data to another country in a way that breaches Applicable Data Protection Law, and must ensure the safeguards the law requires.\n\n## 12. Audit and compliance\n\n12.1 Each party must be able to show compliance with these DP Terms. On reasonable written request (not more than once a year, unless there has been an incident or a regulator requires it) you will give us information about your handling of Customer Data, and we will give you information about the data we process for you (for example our current security summary and third-party attestations or audit reports we hold). Audits, if needed, are by an independent auditor under confidentiality, during business hours and with reasonable notice. [[LEGAL: confirm the audit scope and costs]]\n\n12.2 If we reasonably believe you are using Customer Data in breach of these DP Terms, we may suspend your access to Customer Data, require remediation and, if you do not remedy, close your account.\n\n## 13. Liability\n\n13.1 The limits and exclusions of liability in the Seller Terms apply to these DP Terms. Each party is responsible to the other for breaches of these DP Terms and to individuals as the law provides. You indemnify AfroTransact as set out in the Seller Terms for claims arising from your handling of Customer Data. [[LEGAL: confirm whether data-protection liability should be uncapped or have a super-cap]]\n\n## 14. Changes, term and contact\n\n14.1 These DP Terms run for as long as you have an account and for as long as you hold Customer Data. We may update them under the change provisions of the Seller Terms, and sooner if required by law or by a regulator.\n\n14.2 Contact for privacy matters: {privacy}. Legal notices: {legal}. Address: {address}.\n\n## Annex A. Description of processing when AfroTransact is your processor\n\n| Item | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Subject matter and nature | Hosting and operating Seller Center or Provider Hub for you; storing your team members'' account details, your catalogue and notes; sending messages you ask us to send through the Marketplace. |\n| Purpose | To provide the Marketplace tools to you. |\n| Duration | The life of your account plus the deletion period in section 10. |\n| Types of data | Team member names, work email, phone, role and activity logs; your catalogue content; notes and messages you enter. |\n| Data subjects | Your team members and contractors; individuals named in your notes. |\n| Sub-processors | As on the Sub-processors list. |\n\n## Annex B. AfroTransact security measures (summary)\n\nThe following describes controls at the time of drafting, derived from the platform''s documentation. Operations must confirm each before publication [[LEGAL: operations to verify each statement]].\n- Access control: role-based access, with separation of duties for seller review (two different reviewers) and masked display of sensitive fields; reviewer views of documents and unmasked identifiers are logged.\n- Authentication: central identity service, multi-factor options, step-up for sensitive administrative actions.\n- Encryption: encryption in transit; sensitive identifiers (such as tax identification numbers) stored as masked values with field-level encryption; keys managed separately and rotated.\n- Payment data: card and bank details are collected by Stripe and not stored by AfroTransact.\n- Documents: verification documents are held in a restricted bucket and accessed through signed, logged links.\n- Audit: an append-only, hash-chained audit log of staff decisions.\n- Network and application security: vulnerability scanning, secret scanning, least-privilege service credentials, secrets held in a cloud secret store.\n- Monitoring and incident response: metrics, logs and alerts, with an on-call process.\n- Resilience: backups and restore procedures [[LEGAL: operations to describe backup frequency and testing]].\n- Data minimisation: telemetry is scrubbed of personal data where practicable; handover codes are not stored in clear text in delivery event logs."}'::jsonb, change_summary = 'New document. DPA-style terms for sellers and providers that handle customer data: roles, permitted use, security, incident notice, assistance with rights requests, deletion, sub-processors, international transfers (placeholder) and audit.', created_at = now(), updated_at = now()
    where doc_type = 'data-processing' and (major, minor, patch) = (1, 0, 0) and status = 'CURRENT'
      and created_by in ('seed', 'legal-loader') and updated_at = created_at;

-- seller-fees 1.0 (effective 2026-12-01; locales: en; draft banner on)
delete from legal_version where doc_type = 'seller-fees' and created_by = 'seed'
    and exists (select 1 from legal_version c where c.doc_type = 'seller-fees' and c.status = 'CURRENT'
                and c.created_by = 'seed' and c.updated_at = c.created_at and (c.major, c.minor, c.patch) > (1, 0, 0));
update legal_version set status = 'ARCHIVED', archived_at = now(), updated_at = now()
    where doc_type = 'seller-fees' and status = 'CURRENT' and (major, minor, patch) < (1, 0, 0);
insert into legal_version (id, doc_type, version, major, minor, patch, effective_date, status, draft_banner, bodies,
                           change_summary, created_at, created_by, updated_at, archived_at)
select '5fe64bf0-44b1-5e97-aafd-23699bcdb374'::uuid, 'seller-fees', '1.0', 1, 0, 0, '2026-12-01'::date, 'CURRENT', true,
       '{"en": "# Seller Fees Summary\n\n> DRAFT. This document was prepared with AI assistance and has not been reviewed by qualified counsel. It summarises the Seller Terms; if the two differ, the Seller Terms and Service Provider Terms control. Any number on this page is a labelled example, not a current rate. Text in double square brackets marked LEGAL is an open decision.\n\n## 1. How to use this page\n\n1.1 This page explains in plain language what you pay AfroTransact and how each charge is worked out. It does not list prices. AfroTransact Staff configure plans, commission rates, connection fees and delivery fees, and they can change them. The live numbers that apply to you are always in Seller Center (or Provider Hub) under Plan & billing and the rate card.\n\n1.2 Where this page gives a number, it is marked \"Example\" and uses made-up figures to show the arithmetic.\n\n## 2. The short version\n\nYou may pay up to five kinds of charge:\n\n| Charge | Who pays it | When it is taken |\n|---|---|---|\n| Plan fee | Sellers and providers on a paid plan | On each billing cycle (monthly, quarterly or annual, as offered) |\n| Commission | Sellers, on product sales | When the customer''s payment for the goods is captured |\n| Connection fee | Providers, on service bookings | When the Deposit, Balance or kept amount is captured |\n| Delivery, label and handling fees | Sellers, when the fee rule or the label purchase assigns the cost to them | As set out in section 6 |\n| Stripe fees | Your connected account and the payment allocated to your lines | As Stripe charges them |\n\nAfroTransact Retail, our own first-party retail business, pays no commission or plan fee to AfroTransact.\n\n## 3. Plan fee\n\n3.1 A plan is a subscription with a price per billing cycle, features and limits (for example the number of listings you may have, bulk upload, an image library, coupons and AI tools). Staff create and retire plans. A monthly price of zero is a commission-only plan.\n\n3.2 Plan versions. When Staff change a plan''s price or trial, they publish a new version. Existing subscribers stay on the version they pay until their next renewal or until they accept a migration; a price change reaches you at your next renewal and you will have notice as set out in the Seller Terms.\n\n3.3 Trials, free months and rewards. A plan can include a trial or free months, and Staff may run promotions and reward rules, for example a free period if sales in your first period are below a stated amount, or a bonus for reaching a sales or listing goal. Each has stated conditions, and decisions are made once, at the time stated. Seller Center shows the rule in plain words and what applies to you.\n\n3.4 Charge conditions. A plan can be free until you reach a sales or listing threshold, free while you stay under a threshold, or free for some months and then charged. Seller Center shows the charge terms of your plan.\n\n3.5 Negotiated plans. If AfroTransact agrees a different price or terms with you, it is applied as a private plan for your organisation.\n\n## 4. How a plan fee is paid: credits, then earnings (if you opt in), then card\n\nWhen a plan invoice is created, it is settled in this order:\n\n1. Credits, rewards, coupons and free-period balances on your account are applied first.\n2. If you have chosen \"Pay your plan from your sales\" (and this option is switched on), AfroTransact takes what it can from your eligible pending earnings: money from delivered orders that is not under a dispute and not yet transferred to you. Only up to the amount of the invoice is taken.\n3. Anything that remains is charged to the card or bank account you chose for billing.\n\nYou can change the choice in Seller Center; a change applies from your next invoice. If the invoice is voided or credited, money taken from earnings is given back. The option can be switched off, in which case invoices go to your card. AfroTransact Retail is never billed.\n\n## 5. Commission on product sales\n\n5.1 Commission is a percentage of the value of the goods you sell, taken when the customer''s payment for those goods is captured. It is not charged on orders that are cancelled before capture.\n\n5.2 Which rate applies. Staff configure the rate. When an order is placed, the system picks the first match from this list:\n1. Retail: AfroTransact Retail pays nothing.\n2. A negotiated rate for your organisation (dated, for a type of fee, optionally for a category), if one is active.\n3. The rate for your plan and the product''s category.\n4. The rate for the product''s category.\n5. The default rate.\n\nSeller Center shows which source applied to each category (for example \"Negotiated rate until [date]\", \"Your plan\", \"Category rate\" or \"Standard rate\").\n\n5.3 The rate is recorded on each order line when the order is placed. Later changes to plans, categories or negotiated rates do not change orders already placed.\n\n5.4 Example (made-up numbers). Goods sold for $100.00 with a commission rate of 10 percent: commission is $10.00; your share of the goods before Stripe cost allocation is $90.00.\n\n5.5 Commission is shown on your statements as its own column, separate from other fees.\n\n## 6. Delivery, shipping, labels and handling\n\n6.1 Delivery fees are rules, not code constants. Staff configure delivery fee rules by method: AfroTransact Delivery (by distance band), carrier shipping (rate, handling and a free-shipping threshold), and pickup (often no charge). The rule that applies is shown to the customer at checkout and recorded on the order.\n\n6.2 Who bears the cost. Depending on the fulfilment method and the rule:\n- Carrier labels bought through AfroTransact: the carrier''s cost plus a handling charge are charged to you and deducted from your balance.\n- Fulfilment by AfroTransact: a per-unit fulfilment fee set in the fee schedule (and possibly by plan or negotiated) applies, and AfroTransact''s own shipping may be charged to the customer.\n- AfroTransact Delivery from your own location: a seller-side delivery fee set by the fee rule may be charged to you.\n- Seller-delivered and pickup orders: only any fee the rule provides.\n\n6.3 Delivery runs. A delivery run batches orders for a place into one trip. Customers pay at checkout; if a run is cancelled, every order on it is refunded in full automatically.\n\n6.4 Fault rule on refunds. When a refund involves a delivery that has already been made or attempted, the cost of the trip is allocated to whoever is at fault:\n- If the problem is yours (a wrong, damaged or missing item, or a cancellation by you after pickup), the customer can get the delivery charge back and AfroTransact keeps the seller-side delivery fee.\n- If the customer changed their mind or refused the delivery, the delivery charge is not refundable, and the seller-side fee is given back in proportion to the items refunded.\n- Shipments cancelled before pickup cost nothing.\n\n## 7. Connection fee for service providers\n\n7.1 A connection fee is a percentage of the service money that AfroTransact actually captures for you, taken at the moment of capture. Staff configure it by plan, by service category and by negotiated rate, with the same first-match order as commission.\n\n7.2 When it applies:\n\n| Situation | Connection fee |\n|---|---|\n| Deposit captured at confirmation | On the deposit |\n| Balance or full price captured at completion | On the balance |\n| Late customer cancellation or no-show where you keep the deposit or no-show amount | On the amount you keep |\n| Cancelled or declined before anything is captured | None |\n| Refund | Returned in proportion if the fee schedule says so; otherwise kept by AfroTransact |\n\n7.3 Provider late-cancellation fee. If you cancel late or do not attend, a separate provider fee may be charged to you. Staff configure its amount and timing, and it is shown in Provider Hub.\n\n7.4 Provider Hub shows \"what you keep\" before you accept a request: price less the connection fee and estimated card fees. It is an estimate.\n\n7.5 Example (made-up numbers). A $100.00 service with a $20.00 deposit and an 8 percent connection fee: fee on the deposit $1.60 when captured; on the $80.00 balance $6.40 when captured; total $8.00 before card fees.\n\n## 8. Stripe and payout costs\n\n8.1 Stripe charges processing fees on customer payments and account and payout fees under the Stripe agreements. As configured, your connected account bears Stripe''s account and payout fees, and the processing cost of each payment is allocated across the lines in proportion to their value and shown on your statement. See the Payment Terms.\n\n8.2 Example (made-up numbers). A $100.00 customer payment with a Stripe cost of $3.20 for the whole order is split across the lines; your lines carry your proportional share.\n\n## 9. Taxes\n\n9.1 Sales tax is calculated, collected and remitted by AfroTransact on eligible sales as described in the Seller Terms; it is not part of your commission base and is shown separately at checkout. Fees we charge you may themselves attract tax, which is added where it applies. [[LEGAL: confirm tax treatment and wording once tax registration status is known]]\n\n## 10. How and when amounts reach you\n\nYour net earnings are: the item price and your share of shipping, less commission or connection fee, less the allocated Stripe cost, less any label, plan-fee or other deductions. Net earnings for an order become eligible for transfer after the hold window that follows delivery (or service completion), then you can request a payout. See the Seller Terms and Payment Terms.\n\n## 11. Worked example (made-up numbers)\n\n| Line | Amount |\n|---|---|\n| Goods sold | $100.00 |\n| Shipping charged to the customer and passed to you | $5.00 |\n| Commission at an example 10 percent of goods | -$10.00 |\n| Allocated Stripe cost (example) | -$3.50 |\n| Carrier label cost and handling charged to you (example) | -$6.00 |\n| Net earnings | $85.50 |\n\nSales tax (collected by AfroTransact) is outside this table.\n\n## 12. Changes and disputes\n\n12.1 Fee changes follow the notice rules in the Seller Terms. The current live schedule is in Seller Center.\n\n12.2 If you think a fee is wrong, contact support with the order or invoice number. We will review the rate recorded on that order and correct errors.\n\n12.3 Questions: {legal}."}'::jsonb, 'New document. A human-readable summary of how plan fees, commission, service connection fees, delivery, label and handling fees, and Stripe costs work, how rates are chosen, and how fees behave on refunds. Rates are configured by AfroTransact staff and shown in Seller Center; this page contains no live rates.', now(), 'legal-loader', now(), null
 where not exists (select 1 from legal_version where doc_type = 'seller-fees' and (major, minor, patch) >= (1, 0, 0));
update legal_version set effective_date = '2026-12-01'::date, draft_banner = true,
       bodies = '{"en": "# Seller Fees Summary\n\n> DRAFT. This document was prepared with AI assistance and has not been reviewed by qualified counsel. It summarises the Seller Terms; if the two differ, the Seller Terms and Service Provider Terms control. Any number on this page is a labelled example, not a current rate. Text in double square brackets marked LEGAL is an open decision.\n\n## 1. How to use this page\n\n1.1 This page explains in plain language what you pay AfroTransact and how each charge is worked out. It does not list prices. AfroTransact Staff configure plans, commission rates, connection fees and delivery fees, and they can change them. The live numbers that apply to you are always in Seller Center (or Provider Hub) under Plan & billing and the rate card.\n\n1.2 Where this page gives a number, it is marked \"Example\" and uses made-up figures to show the arithmetic.\n\n## 2. The short version\n\nYou may pay up to five kinds of charge:\n\n| Charge | Who pays it | When it is taken |\n|---|---|---|\n| Plan fee | Sellers and providers on a paid plan | On each billing cycle (monthly, quarterly or annual, as offered) |\n| Commission | Sellers, on product sales | When the customer''s payment for the goods is captured |\n| Connection fee | Providers, on service bookings | When the Deposit, Balance or kept amount is captured |\n| Delivery, label and handling fees | Sellers, when the fee rule or the label purchase assigns the cost to them | As set out in section 6 |\n| Stripe fees | Your connected account and the payment allocated to your lines | As Stripe charges them |\n\nAfroTransact Retail, our own first-party retail business, pays no commission or plan fee to AfroTransact.\n\n## 3. Plan fee\n\n3.1 A plan is a subscription with a price per billing cycle, features and limits (for example the number of listings you may have, bulk upload, an image library, coupons and AI tools). Staff create and retire plans. A monthly price of zero is a commission-only plan.\n\n3.2 Plan versions. When Staff change a plan''s price or trial, they publish a new version. Existing subscribers stay on the version they pay until their next renewal or until they accept a migration; a price change reaches you at your next renewal and you will have notice as set out in the Seller Terms.\n\n3.3 Trials, free months and rewards. A plan can include a trial or free months, and Staff may run promotions and reward rules, for example a free period if sales in your first period are below a stated amount, or a bonus for reaching a sales or listing goal. Each has stated conditions, and decisions are made once, at the time stated. Seller Center shows the rule in plain words and what applies to you.\n\n3.4 Charge conditions. A plan can be free until you reach a sales or listing threshold, free while you stay under a threshold, or free for some months and then charged. Seller Center shows the charge terms of your plan.\n\n3.5 Negotiated plans. If AfroTransact agrees a different price or terms with you, it is applied as a private plan for your organisation.\n\n## 4. How a plan fee is paid: credits, then earnings (if you opt in), then card\n\nWhen a plan invoice is created, it is settled in this order:\n\n1. Credits, rewards, coupons and free-period balances on your account are applied first.\n2. If you have chosen \"Pay your plan from your sales\" (and this option is switched on), AfroTransact takes what it can from your eligible pending earnings: money from delivered orders that is not under a dispute and not yet transferred to you. Only up to the amount of the invoice is taken.\n3. Anything that remains is charged to the card or bank account you chose for billing.\n\nYou can change the choice in Seller Center; a change applies from your next invoice. If the invoice is voided or credited, money taken from earnings is given back. The option can be switched off, in which case invoices go to your card. AfroTransact Retail is never billed.\n\n## 5. Commission on product sales\n\n5.1 Commission is a percentage of the value of the goods you sell, taken when the customer''s payment for those goods is captured. It is not charged on orders that are cancelled before capture.\n\n5.2 Which rate applies. Staff configure the rate. When an order is placed, the system picks the first match from this list:\n1. Retail: AfroTransact Retail pays nothing.\n2. A negotiated rate for your organisation (dated, for a type of fee, optionally for a category), if one is active.\n3. The rate for your plan and the product''s category.\n4. The rate for the product''s category.\n5. The default rate.\n\nSeller Center shows which source applied to each category (for example \"Negotiated rate until [date]\", \"Your plan\", \"Category rate\" or \"Standard rate\").\n\n5.3 The rate is recorded on each order line when the order is placed. Later changes to plans, categories or negotiated rates do not change orders already placed.\n\n5.4 Example (made-up numbers). Goods sold for $100.00 with a commission rate of 10 percent: commission is $10.00; your share of the goods before Stripe cost allocation is $90.00.\n\n5.5 Commission is shown on your statements as its own column, separate from other fees.\n\n## 6. Delivery, shipping, labels and handling\n\n6.1 Delivery fees are rules, not code constants. Staff configure delivery fee rules by method: AfroTransact Delivery (by distance band), carrier shipping (rate, handling and a free-shipping threshold), and pickup (often no charge). The rule that applies is shown to the customer at checkout and recorded on the order.\n\n6.2 Who bears the cost. Depending on the fulfilment method and the rule:\n- Carrier labels bought through AfroTransact: the carrier''s cost plus a handling charge are charged to you and deducted from your balance.\n- Fulfilment by AfroTransact: a per-unit fulfilment fee set in the fee schedule (and possibly by plan or negotiated) applies, and AfroTransact''s own shipping may be charged to the customer.\n- AfroTransact Delivery from your own location: a seller-side delivery fee set by the fee rule may be charged to you.\n- Seller-delivered and pickup orders: only any fee the rule provides.\n\n6.3 Delivery runs. A delivery run batches orders for a place into one trip. Customers pay at checkout; if a run is cancelled, every order on it is refunded in full automatically.\n\n6.4 Fault rule on refunds. When a refund involves a delivery that has already been made or attempted, the cost of the trip is allocated to whoever is at fault:\n- If the problem is yours (a wrong, damaged or missing item, or a cancellation by you after pickup), the customer can get the delivery charge back and AfroTransact keeps the seller-side delivery fee.\n- If the customer changed their mind or refused the delivery, the delivery charge is not refundable, and the seller-side fee is given back in proportion to the items refunded.\n- Shipments cancelled before pickup cost nothing.\n\n## 7. Connection fee for service providers\n\n7.1 A connection fee is a percentage of the service money that AfroTransact actually captures for you, taken at the moment of capture. Staff configure it by plan, by service category and by negotiated rate, with the same first-match order as commission.\n\n7.2 When it applies:\n\n| Situation | Connection fee |\n|---|---|\n| Deposit captured at confirmation | On the deposit |\n| Balance or full price captured at completion | On the balance |\n| Late customer cancellation or no-show where you keep the deposit or no-show amount | On the amount you keep |\n| Cancelled or declined before anything is captured | None |\n| Refund | Returned in proportion if the fee schedule says so; otherwise kept by AfroTransact |\n\n7.3 Provider late-cancellation fee. If you cancel late or do not attend, a separate provider fee may be charged to you. Staff configure its amount and timing, and it is shown in Provider Hub.\n\n7.4 Provider Hub shows \"what you keep\" before you accept a request: price less the connection fee and estimated card fees. It is an estimate.\n\n7.5 Example (made-up numbers). A $100.00 service with a $20.00 deposit and an 8 percent connection fee: fee on the deposit $1.60 when captured; on the $80.00 balance $6.40 when captured; total $8.00 before card fees.\n\n## 8. Stripe and payout costs\n\n8.1 Stripe charges processing fees on customer payments and account and payout fees under the Stripe agreements. As configured, your connected account bears Stripe''s account and payout fees, and the processing cost of each payment is allocated across the lines in proportion to their value and shown on your statement. See the Payment Terms.\n\n8.2 Example (made-up numbers). A $100.00 customer payment with a Stripe cost of $3.20 for the whole order is split across the lines; your lines carry your proportional share.\n\n## 9. Taxes\n\n9.1 Sales tax is calculated, collected and remitted by AfroTransact on eligible sales as described in the Seller Terms; it is not part of your commission base and is shown separately at checkout. Fees we charge you may themselves attract tax, which is added where it applies. [[LEGAL: confirm tax treatment and wording once tax registration status is known]]\n\n## 10. How and when amounts reach you\n\nYour net earnings are: the item price and your share of shipping, less commission or connection fee, less the allocated Stripe cost, less any label, plan-fee or other deductions. Net earnings for an order become eligible for transfer after the hold window that follows delivery (or service completion), then you can request a payout. See the Seller Terms and Payment Terms.\n\n## 11. Worked example (made-up numbers)\n\n| Line | Amount |\n|---|---|\n| Goods sold | $100.00 |\n| Shipping charged to the customer and passed to you | $5.00 |\n| Commission at an example 10 percent of goods | -$10.00 |\n| Allocated Stripe cost (example) | -$3.50 |\n| Carrier label cost and handling charged to you (example) | -$6.00 |\n| Net earnings | $85.50 |\n\nSales tax (collected by AfroTransact) is outside this table.\n\n## 12. Changes and disputes\n\n12.1 Fee changes follow the notice rules in the Seller Terms. The current live schedule is in Seller Center.\n\n12.2 If you think a fee is wrong, contact support with the order or invoice number. We will review the rate recorded on that order and correct errors.\n\n12.3 Questions: {legal}."}'::jsonb, change_summary = 'New document. A human-readable summary of how plan fees, commission, service connection fees, delivery, label and handling fees, and Stripe costs work, how rates are chosen, and how fees behave on refunds. Rates are configured by AfroTransact staff and shown in Seller Center; this page contains no live rates.', created_at = now(), updated_at = now()
    where doc_type = 'seller-fees' and (major, minor, patch) = (1, 0, 0) and status = 'CURRENT'
      and created_by in ('seed', 'legal-loader') and updated_at = created_at;
